papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:179: trailing whitespace.
+1.2 Scope and Direction 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:180: trailing whitespace.
+The focus of this guidance is on statistical princi ples. It does not address the use of specific 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:181: trailing whitespace.
+statistical procedures or methods. Specific proc edural steps to ensure that principles are 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:182: trailing whitespace.
+implemented properly are the responsibility of th e sponsor. Integration of data across clinical 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:183: trailing whitespace.
+trials is discussed, but is not a primary focu s of this guidance. Selected principles and 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:184: trailing whitespace.
+procedures related to data management or clin ical trial monitoring activities are covered in 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:185: trailing whitespace.
+other ICH guidelines and are not addressed here. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:186: trailing whitespace.
+This guidance should be of interest to individual s from a broad range of scientific disciplines. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:187: trailing whitespace.
+However, it is assumed that the actual responsib ility for all statistical work associated with 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:188: trailing whitespace.
+clinical trials will lie with an appropriately qualified and experienced statistician, as indicated 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:189: trailing whitespace.
+in ICH E6. The role and responsibility of the tria l statistician (see Glossary), in collaboration 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:190: trailing whitespace.
+with other clinical trial professionals, is to ensure that statistical principles are applied 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:191: trailing whitespace.
+appropriately in clinical trials supporting drug de velopment. Thus, the trial statistician should 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:192: trailing whitespace.
+have a combination of education/training and experience sufficient to implement the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:193: trailing whitespace.
+principles articulated in this guidance. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:194: trailing whitespace.
+For each clinical trial contributing to a market ing application, all important details of its 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:195: trailing whitespace.
+design and conduct and the principal features of  its proposed statistical analysis should be 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:196: trailing whitespace.
+clearly specified in a protocol written before  the trial begins. The extent to which the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:197: trailing whitespace.
+procedures in the protocol are followed and the primary analysis is planned a priori will 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:198: trailing whitespace.
+contribute to the degree of confidence in the final results and conclusions of the trial. The 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:199: trailing whitespace.
+protocol and subsequent amendments should be approved by the responsible personnel, 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:200: trailing whitespace.
+including the trial statistician. The trial statis tician should ensure that the protocol and any 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:201: trailing whitespace.
+amendments cover all relevant statistical issues clearly and accurately, using technical 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:202: trailing whitespace.
+terminology as appropriate. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:203: trailing whitespace.
+The principles outlined in this guidance are primar ily relevant to clinical trials conducted in 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:204: trailing whitespace.
+the later phases of development, many of which are confirmatory trials of efficacy. In addition 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:205: trailing whitespace.
+to efficacy, confirmatory trials may have as th eir primary variable a safety variable (e.g. an 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:206: trailing whitespace.
+adverse event, a clinical laboratory variab le or an electrocardiographic measure), a 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:207: trailing whitespace.
+pharmacodynamic or a pharmacokinetic variable (as in a confirmatory bioequivalence trial). 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:208: trailing whitespace.
+Furthermore, some confirmatory findings may be derived from data integrated across trials, 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:209: trailing whitespace.
+and selected principles in this guidance are ap plicable in this situation. Finally, although the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:210: trailing whitespace.
+early phases of drug development consist mainly of clinical trials that are exploratory in 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:211: trailing whitespace.
+nature, statistical principles are also relevant to these clinical trials. Hence, the substance of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:212: trailing whitespace.
+this document should be applied as far as possible to all phases of clinical development. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:213: trailing whitespace.
+Many of the principles delineated in this guida nce deal with minimising bias (see Glossary) 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:214: trailing whitespace.
+and maximising precision. As used in this guid ance, the term 'bias' describes the systematic 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:215: trailing whitespace.
+tendency of any factors associated with the desi gn, conduct, analysis and interpretation of the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:216: trailing whitespace.
+results of clinical trials to make the estimate of a treatment effect (see Glossary) deviate from 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:217: trailing whitespace.
+its true value. It is important to identify potenti al sources of bias as completely as possible so 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:218: trailing whitespace.
+that attempts to limit such bias may be made. The presence of bias may seriously compromise 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:219: trailing whitespace.
+the ability to draw valid conclusions from clinical trials. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:220: trailing whitespace.
+Some sources of bias arise from the design of the trial, for example an assignment of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:221: trailing whitespace.
+treatments such that subjects at lower risk are systematically assigned to one treatment. Other 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:222: trailing whitespace.
+sources of bias arise during the conduct and analys is of a clinical trial. For example, protocol 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:223: trailing whitespace.
+violations and exclusion of subjects from analysis based upon knowledge of subject outcomes 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:224: trailing whitespace.
+are possible sources of bias that may affect the accurate assessment of the treatment effect. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:225: trailing whitespace.
+Because bias can occur in subtle or unknown ways and its effect is not measurable directly, it 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:226: trailing whitespace.
+is important to evaluate the robustness of the results and primary conclusions of the trial. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:227: trailing whitespace.
+Robustness is a concept that refers to the sensitivity of the overall conclusions to various 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:228: trailing whitespace.
+limitations of the data, assumptions, and anal ytic approaches to data analysis. Robustness 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:232: trailing whitespace.
+Â© EMEA 2006  6 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:233: trailing whitespace.
+ 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:234: trailing whitespace.
+implies that the treatment effect and primary conclusions of the trial are not substantially 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:235: trailing whitespace.
+affected when analyses are carried out based on alternative assumptions or analytic 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:236: trailing whitespace.
+approaches. The interpretation of statistical meas ures of uncertainty of the treatment effect 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:237: trailing whitespace.
+and treatment comparisons should involve consider ation of the potential contribution of bias 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:238: trailing whitespace.
+to the p-value, confidence interval, or inference. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:239: trailing whitespace.
+Because the predominant approaches to the design and analysis of clinical trials have been 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:240: trailing whitespace.
+based on frequentist statistical me thods, the guidance largely refers to the use of frequentist 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:241: trailing whitespace.
+methods (see Glossary) when discussing hypothesi s testing and/or confidence intervals. This 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:242: trailing whitespace.
+should not be taken to imply that other approaches are not appropriate: the use of Bayesian 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:243: trailing whitespace.
+(see Glossary) and other approaches may be considered when the reasons for their use are 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:244: trailing whitespace.
+clear and when the resulting conclusions are sufficiently robust. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:245: trailing whitespace.
+II CONSIDERATIONS FOR OVERALL CLINICAL DEVELOPMENT 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:246: trailing whitespace.
+2.1 Trial Context 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:247: trailing whitespace.
+2.1.1 Development Plan 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:248: trailing whitespace.
+The broad aim of the process of clinical development of a new drug is to find out whether 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:249: trailing whitespace.
+there is a dose range and schedule at which the drug can be shown to be simultaneously safe 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:250: trailing whitespace.
+and effective, to the extent that the risk-benefit relationship is acceptable. The particular 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:251: trailing whitespace.
+subjects who may benefit from the drug, and the specific indications for its use, also need to 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:252: trailing whitespace.
+be defined. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:253: trailing whitespace.
+Satisfying these broad aims usually requires an ordered programme of clinical trials, each 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:254: trailing whitespace.
+with its own specific objectives (see ICH E8). Th is should be specified in a clinical plan, or a 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:255: trailing whitespace.
+series of plans, with appropriate decision po ints and flexibility to allow modification as 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:256: trailing whitespace.
+knowledge accumulates. A marketing application sh ould clearly describe the main content of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:257: trailing whitespace.
+such plans, and the contribution made by each trial. Interpretation and assessment of the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:258: trailing whitespace.
+evidence from the total programme of trials involves synthesis of the evidence from the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:259: trailing whitespace.
+individual trials (see Section 7.2). This is facilitated by ensuring that common standards are 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:260: trailing whitespace.
+adopted for a number of features of the trials su ch as dictionaries of medical terms, definition 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:261: trailing whitespace.
+and timing of the main measurements, handling of protocol deviations and so on. A statistical 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:262: trailing whitespace.
+summary, overview or meta-analysis (see Glos sary) may be informative when medical 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:263: trailing whitespace.
+questions are addressed in more than one trial.  Where possible this should be envisaged in the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:264: trailing whitespace.
+plan so that the relevant trials are clearly identified and any necessary common features of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:265: trailing whitespace.
+their designs are specified in advance. Other major statistical issues (if any) that are expected 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:266: trailing whitespace.
+to affect a number of trials in a common plan should be addressed in that plan. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:267: trailing whitespace.
+2.1.2 Confirmatory Trial 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:268: trailing whitespace.
+A confirmatory trial is an adequately controlled trial in which the hypotheses are stated in 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:269: trailing whitespace.
+advance and evaluated. As a rule, confirmatory trials are necessary to provide firm evidence 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:270: trailing whitespace.
+of efficacy or safety. In such trials the key hypothesis of interest follows directly from the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:271: trailing whitespace.
+trialâ€™s primary objective, is always pre-defined,  and is the hypothesis that is subsequently 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:272: trailing whitespace.
+tested when the trial is complete. In a confirmatory trial it is equally important to estimate 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:273: trailing whitespace.
+with due precision the size of the effects attributable to the treatment of interest and to relate 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:274: trailing whitespace.
+these effects to their clinical significance. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:275: trailing whitespace.
+Confirmatory trials are intended to provide firm evidence in support of claims and hence 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:276: trailing whitespace.
+adherence to protocols and standard operati ng procedures is particularly important; 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:277: trailing whitespace.
+unavoidable changes should be explained and documented, and their effect examined. A 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:278: trailing whitespace.
+justification of the design of each such trial, and of other important statistical aspects such as 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:282: trailing whitespace.
+Â© EMEA 2006  7 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:283: trailing whitespace.
+ 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:284: trailing whitespace.
+the principal features of the planned analysis, should be set out in the protocol. Each trial 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:285: trailing whitespace.
+should address only a limited number of questions. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:286: trailing whitespace.
+Firm evidence in support of claims requires that the results of the confirmatory trials 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:287: trailing whitespace.
+demonstrate that the investigational product under  test has clinical benefits. The confirmatory 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:288: trailing whitespace.
+trials should therefore be sufficient to answer  each key clinical question relevant to the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:289: trailing whitespace.
+efficacy or safety claim clearly and definitively. In addition, it is important that the basis for 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:290: trailing whitespace.
+generalisation (see Glossary) to the intended patient population is understood and explained; 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:291: trailing whitespace.
+this may also influence the number and type (e.g . specialist or general practitioner) of centres 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:292: trailing whitespace.
+and/or trials needed. The results of the conf irmatory trial(s) should be robust. In some 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:293: trailing whitespace.
+circumstances the weight of evidence from a single confirmatory trial may be sufficient. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:294: trailing whitespace.
+2.1.3 Exploratory Trial 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:295: trailing whitespace.
+The rationale and design of confirmatory trials nearly always rests on earlier clinical work 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:296: trailing whitespace.
+carried out in a series of exploratory studies. Like all clinical trials, these exploratory studies 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:297: trailing whitespace.
+should have clear and precise objectives. However, in contrast to confirmatory trials, their 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:298: trailing whitespace.
+objectives may not always lead to simple te sts of pre-defined hypotheses. In addition, 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:299: trailing whitespace.
+exploratory trials may sometimes require a more flexible approach to design so that changes 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:300: trailing whitespace.
+can be made in response to accumulating results . Their analysis may entail data exploration; 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:301: trailing whitespace.
+tests of hypothesis may be carried out, but the choice of hypothesis may be data dependent. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:302: trailing whitespace.
+Such trials cannot be the basis of the formal proof of efficacy, although they may contribute 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:303: trailing whitespace.
+to the total body of relevant evidence. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:304: trailing whitespace.
+Any individual trial may have both confirmatory  and exploratory aspects. For example, in 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:305: trailing whitespace.
+most confirmatory trials the data are also subj ected to exploratory analyses which serve as a 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:306: trailing whitespace.
+basis for explaining or supporting their findin gs and for suggesting further hypotheses for 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:307: trailing whitespace.
+later research. The protocol should make a clea r distinction between the aspects of a trial 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:308: trailing whitespace.
+which will be used for confirmatory proof and the aspects which will provide data for 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:309: trailing whitespace.
+exploratory analysis. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:310: trailing whitespace.
+2.2 Scope of Trials 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:311: trailing whitespace.
+2.2.1 Population 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:312: trailing whitespace.
+In the earlier phases of drug development the choice of subjects for a clinical trial may be 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:313: trailing whitespace.
+heavily influenced by the wish to maximise the chance of observing specific clinical effects of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:314: trailing whitespace.
+interest, and hence they may come from a very narrow subgroup of the total patient 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:315: trailing whitespace.
+population for which the drug may eventually be indicated. However by the time the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:316: trailing whitespace.
+confirmatory trials are undertaken, the subjects in the trials should more closely mirror the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:317: trailing whitespace.
+target population. Hence, in these trials it is generally helpful to relax the inclusion and 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:318: trailing whitespace.
+exclusion criteria as much as possible within the target population, while maintaining 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:319: trailing whitespace.
+sufficient homogeneity to permit precise estimation of treatment effects. No individual 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:320: trailing whitespace.
+clinical trial can be expected to be totally representative of future users, because of the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:321: trailing whitespace.
+possible influences of geographical location, th e time when it is conducted, the medical 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:322: trailing whitespace.
+practices of the particular investigator(s) and clinics, and so on. However the influence of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:323: trailing whitespace.
+such factors should be reduced wherever possi ble, and subsequently discussed during the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:324: trailing whitespace.
+interpretation of the trial results. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:325: trailing whitespace.
+2.2.2 Primary and Secondary Variables 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:326: trailing whitespace.
+The primary variable (â€˜targetâ€™ variable, primar y endpoint) should be the variable capable of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:327: trailing whitespace.
+providing the most clinically relevant and convin cing evidence directly related to the primary 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:328: trailing whitespace.
+objective of the trial. There should generally be only one primary variable. This will usually 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:329: trailing whitespace.
+be an efficacy variable, because the primary objective of most confirmatory trials is to 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:333: trailing whitespace.
+Â© EMEA 2006  8 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:334: trailing whitespace.
+ 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:335: trailing whitespace.
+provide strong scientific evidence regarding e fficacy. Safety/tolerability may sometimes be 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:336: trailing whitespace.
+the primary variable, and will always be an important consideration. Measurements relating to 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:337: trailing whitespace.
+quality of life and health economics are furthe r potential primary variables. The selection of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:338: trailing whitespace.
+the primary variable should reflect the accepted norms and standards in the relevant field of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:339: trailing whitespace.
+research. The use of a reliable and validated variable with which experience has been gained 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:340: trailing whitespace.
+either in earlier studies or in  published literature is recommended. There should be sufficient 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:341: trailing whitespace.
+evidence that the primary variable can provide a valid and reliable measure of some clinically 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:342: trailing whitespace.
+relevant and important treatment benefit in the patient population described by the inclusion 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:343: trailing whitespace.
+and exclusion criteria. The primary variable should generally be the one used when estimating 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:344: trailing whitespace.
+the sample size (see section 3.5). 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:345: trailing whitespace.
+In many cases, the approach to assessing subject outcome may not be straightforward and 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:346: trailing whitespace.
+should be carefully defined. For example, it is inadequate to specify mortality as a primary 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:347: trailing whitespace.
+variable without further clarification; mortal ity may be assessed by comparing proportions 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:348: trailing whitespace.
+alive at fixed points in time, or by comparing overall distributions of survival times over a 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:349: trailing whitespace.
+specified interval. Another common example is a recurring event; the measure of treatment 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:350: trailing whitespace.
+effect may again be a simple dichotomous va riable (any occurrence during a specified 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:351: trailing whitespace.
+interval), time to first occurrence, rate of occurrence (events per time units of observation), 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:352: trailing whitespace.
+etc. The assessment of functional status over time in studying treatment for chronic disease 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:353: trailing whitespace.
+presents other challenges in selection of the primary variable. There are many possible 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:354: trailing whitespace.
+approaches, such as comparisons of the asse ssments done at the beginning and end of the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:355: trailing whitespace.
+interval of observation, comparisons of slopes calculated from all assessments throughout the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:356: trailing whitespace.
+interval, comparisons of the proportions of subjects exceeding or declining beyond a specified 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:357: trailing whitespace.
+threshold, or comparisons based on methods for repeated measures data. To avoid multiplicity 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:358: trailing whitespace.
+concerns arising from post hoc definitions, it is cr itical to specify in the protocol the precise 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:359: trailing whitespace.
+definition of the primary variable as it will be used in the statistical analysis. In addition, the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:360: trailing whitespace.
+clinical relevance of the specific primary variable selected and the validity of the associated 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:361: trailing whitespace.
+measurement procedures will generally need to be addressed and justified in the protocol. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:362: trailing whitespace.
+The primary variable should be specified in the protocol, along with the rationale for its 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:363: trailing whitespace.
+selection. Redefinition of the primary variab le after unblinding will almost always be 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:364: trailing whitespace.
+unacceptable, since the biases this introduces are difficult to assess. When the clinical effect 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:365: trailing whitespace.
+defined by the primary objective is to be measured in more than one way, the protocol should 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:366: trailing whitespace.
+identify one of the measurements as the primary variable on the basis of clinical relevance, 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:367: trailing whitespace.
+importance, objectivity, and/or other relevant characteristics, whenever such selection is 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:368: trailing whitespace.
+feasible. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:369: trailing whitespace.
+Secondary variables are either supportive measur ements related to the primary objective or 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:370: trailing whitespace.
+measurements of effects related to the second ary objectives. Their pre-definition in the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:371: trailing whitespace.
+protocol is also important, as well as an explan ation of their relative importance and roles in 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:372: trailing whitespace.
+interpretation of trial results. The number of secondary variables should be limited and should 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:373: trailing whitespace.
+be related to the limited number of questions to be answered in the trial. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:374: trailing whitespace.
+2.2.3 Composite Variables 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:375: trailing whitespace.
+If a single primary variable cannot be selected from multiple measurements associated with 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:376: trailing whitespace.
+the primary objective, another useful strategy is to integrate or combine the multiple 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:377: trailing whitespace.
+measurements into a single or 'composite' vari able, using a pre-defined algorithm. Indeed, the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:378: trailing whitespace.
+primary variable sometimes arises as a combinat ion of multiple clinical measurements (e.g. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:379: trailing whitespace.
+the rating scales used in arthritis, psychiatric disorders and elsewhere). This approach 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:380: trailing whitespace.
+addresses the multiplicity problem without requiring adjustment to the type I error. The 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:381: trailing whitespace.
+method of combining the multiple measurements should be specified in the protocol, and an 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:382: trailing whitespace.
+interpretation of the resulting scale should be provided in terms of the size of a clinically 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:383: trailing whitespace.
+relevant benefit. When a composite variable is used as a primary variable, the components of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:384: trailing whitespace.
+this variable may sometimes be analysed separately, where clinically meaningful and 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:388: trailing whitespace.
+Â© EMEA 2006  9 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:389: trailing whitespace.
+ 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:390: trailing whitespace.
+validated. When a rating scale is used as a pr imary variable, it is especially important to 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:391: trailing whitespace.
+address such factors as content validity (see Glos sary), inter- and intra-rater reliability (see 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:392: trailing whitespace.
+Glossary) and responsiveness for detecting changes in the severity of disease. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:393: trailing whitespace.
+ 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:394: trailing whitespace.
+2.2.4 Global Assessment Variables 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:395: trailing whitespace.
+In some cases, 'global assessment' variables (see Glossary) are developed to measure the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:396: trailing whitespace.
+overall safety, overall efficacy, and/or overall usef ulness of a treatment. This type of variable 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:397: trailing whitespace.
+integrates objective variables and the investigat orâ€™s overall impression about the state or 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:398: trailing whitespace.
+change in the state of the subject, and is usua lly a scale of ordered ca tegorical ratings. Global 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:399: trailing whitespace.
+assessments of overall efficacy are well established in some therapeutic areas, such as 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:400: trailing whitespace.
+neurology and psychiatry. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:401: trailing whitespace.
+Global assessment variables generally have a subjective component. When a global 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:402: trailing whitespace.
+assessment variable is used as a primary or secondary variable, fuller details of the scale 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:403: trailing whitespace.
+should be included in the protocol with respect to: 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:404: trailing whitespace.
+1. the relevance of the scale to the primary objective of the trial; 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:405: trailing whitespace.
+2. the basis for the validity and reliability of the scale; 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:406: trailing whitespace.
+3. how to utilise the data collected on an individual subject to assign him/her to a unique 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:407: trailing whitespace.
+category of the scale; 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:408: trailing whitespace.
+4. how to assign subjects with missing data to  a unique category of the scale, or otherwise 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:409: trailing whitespace.
+evaluate them. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:410: trailing whitespace.
+If objective variables are considered by the i nvestigator when making a global assessment, 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:411: trailing whitespace.
+then those objective variables should be considered as additional primary, or at least 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:412: trailing whitespace.
+important secondary, variables. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:413: trailing whitespace.
+Global assessment of usefulness integrates co mponents of both benefit and risk and reflects 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:414: trailing whitespace.
+the decision making process of the treating phy sician, who must weigh benefit and risk in 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:415: trailing whitespace.
+making product use decisions. A problem with gl obal usefulness variables is that their use 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:416: trailing whitespace.
+could in some cases lead to the result of tw o products being declared equivalent despite 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:417: trailing whitespace.
+having very different profiles of beneficial and adverse effects. For example, judging the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:418: trailing whitespace.
+global usefulness of a treatment as equivalent or  superior to an alternative may mask the fact 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:419: trailing whitespace.
+that it has little or no efficacy  but fewer adverse effects. Therefore it is not advisable to use a 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:420: trailing whitespace.
+global usefulness variable as a primary variable. If global usefulness is specified as primary, it 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:421: trailing whitespace.
+is important to consider specific efficacy and safety outcomes separately as additional primary 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:422: trailing whitespace.
+variables. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:423: trailing whitespace.
+2.2.5 Multiple Primary Variables 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:424: trailing whitespace.
+It may sometimes be desirable to use more than one primary variable, each of which (or a 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:425: trailing whitespace.
+subset of which) could be sufficient to cover the range of effects of the therapies. The planned 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:426: trailing whitespace.
+manner of interpretation of this type of eviden ce should be carefully spelled out. It should be 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:427: trailing whitespace.
+clear whether an impact on any of the variable s, some minimum number of them, or all of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:428: trailing whitespace.
+them, would be considered necessary to achieve the trial objectives. The primary hypothesis 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:429: trailing whitespace.
+or hypotheses and parameters of interest (e .g. mean, percentage, distribution) should be 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:430: trailing whitespace.
+clearly stated with respect to the primary variab les identified, and the approach to statistical 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:431: trailing whitespace.
+inference described. The effect on the type I error should be explained because of the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:432: trailing whitespace.
+potential for multiplicity problems (see Section 5.6); the method of controlling type I error 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:433: trailing whitespace.
+should be given in the protocol. The extent of intercorrelation among the proposed primary 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:434: trailing whitespace.
+variables may be considered in evaluating the impact on type I error. If the purpose of the trial 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:435: trailing whitespace.
+is to demonstrate effects on all of the designated primary variables, then there is no need for 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:439: trailing whitespace.
+Â© EMEA 2006  10 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:440: trailing whitespace.
+ 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:441: trailing whitespace.
+adjustment of the type I error, but the impact  on type II error and sample size should be 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:442: trailing whitespace.
+carefully considered. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:443: trailing whitespace.
+2.2.6 Surrogate Variables 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:444: trailing whitespace.
+When direct assessment of the clinical benef it to the subject through observing actual clinical 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:445: trailing whitespace.
+efficacy is not practical, indirect criteria (surrogate variables - see Glossary) may be 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:446: trailing whitespace.
+considered. Commonly accepted surrogate variables are used in a number of indications 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:447: trailing whitespace.
+where they are believed to be reliable predicto rs of clinical benefit. There are two principal 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:448: trailing whitespace.
+concerns with the introduction of any proposed surrogate variable. First, it may not be a true 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:449: trailing whitespace.
+predictor of the clinical outcome of interest. For example it may measure treatment activity 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:450: trailing whitespace.
+associated with one specific pharmacological mechanism, but may not provide full 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:451: trailing whitespace.
+information on the range of actions and ultimate effects of the treatment, whether positive or 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:452: trailing whitespace.
+negative. There have been many instances where treatments showing a highly positive effect 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:453: trailing whitespace.
+on a proposed surrogate have ultimately been shown to be detrimental to the subjects' clinical 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:454: trailing whitespace.
+outcome; conversely, there are cases of treatments conferring clinical benefit without 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:455: trailing whitespace.
+measurable impact on proposed surrogates. Secondly, proposed surrogate variables may not 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:456: trailing whitespace.
+yield a quantitative measure of clinical benefit that can be weighed directly against adverse 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:457: trailing whitespace.
+effects. Statistical criteria for validating su rrogate variables have been proposed but the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:458: trailing whitespace.
+experience with their use is relatively limited. In practice, the strength of the evidence for 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:459: trailing whitespace.
+surrogacy depends upon (i) the biological plau sibility of the relationship, (ii) the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:460: trailing whitespace.
+demonstration in epidemiological studies of the prognostic value of the surrogate for the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:461: trailing whitespace.
+clinical outcome and (iii) evidence from clinical trials that treatment effects on the surrogate 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:462: trailing whitespace.
+correspond to effects on the clinical outcome. Relationships between clinical and surrogate 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:463: trailing whitespace.
+variables for one product do not necessarily apply to a product with a different mode of action 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:464: trailing whitespace.
+for treating the same disease. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:465: trailing whitespace.
+2.2.7   Categorised Variables 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:466: trailing whitespace.
+Dichotomisation or other categorisation of cont inuous or ordinal variables may sometimes be 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:467: trailing whitespace.
+desirable. Criteria of 'success' and 'response' are common examples of dichotomies which 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:468: trailing whitespace.
+require precise specification in terms of, for example, a minimum percentage improvement 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:469: trailing whitespace.
+(relative to baseline) in a continuous variable, or  a ranking categorised as at or above some 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:470: trailing whitespace.
+threshold level (e.g., 'good') on an ordinal rating scale. The reduction of diastolic blood 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:471: trailing whitespace.
+pressure below 90mmHg is a common dichotomisat ion. Categorisations are most useful when 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:472: trailing whitespace.
+they have clear clinical relevance. The crite ria for categorisation s hould be pre-defined and 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:473: trailing whitespace.
+specified in the protocol, as knowledge of trial results could easily bias the choice of such 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:474: trailing whitespace.
+criteria. Because categorisation normally implies a loss of information, a consequence will be 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:475: trailing whitespace.
+a loss of power in the analysis; this should be accounted for in the sample size calculation.  
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:476: trailing whitespace.
+2.3 Design Techniques to Avoid Bias 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:477: trailing whitespace.
+The most important design techniques for avoiding bias in clinical trials are blinding and 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:478: trailing whitespace.
+randomisation, and these should be normal features of most controlled clinical trials intended 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:479: trailing whitespace.
+to be included in a marketing application. Most such trials follow a double-blind approach in 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:480: trailing whitespace.
+which treatments are pre-packed in accordance with a suitable randomisation schedule, and 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:481: trailing whitespace.
+supplied to the trial centre(s) labelled only with the subject number and the treatment period 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:482: trailing whitespace.
+so that no one involved in the conduct of the trial is aware of the specific treatment allocated 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:483: trailing whitespace.
+to any particular subject, not even as a code letter. This approach will be assumed in Section 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:484: trailing whitespace.
+2.3.1 and most of Section 2.3.2, exceptions being considered at the end.  
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:485: trailing whitespace.
+Bias can also be reduced at the design stage by specifying procedures in the protocol aimed at 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:486: trailing whitespace.
+minimising any anticipated irregularities in tria l conduct that might impair a satisfactory 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:487: trailing whitespace.
+analysis, including various types of protocol violations, withdrawals and missing values. The 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:491: trailing whitespace.
+Â© EMEA 2006  11 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:492: trailing whitespace.
+ 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:493: trailing whitespace.
+protocol should consider ways both to reduce the frequency of such problems, and also to 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:494: trailing whitespace.
+handle the problems that do occur in the analysis of data.  
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:495: trailing whitespace.
+2.3.1 Blinding 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:496: trailing whitespace.
+Blinding or masking is intended to limit the occurrence of conscious and unconscious bias in 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:497: trailing whitespace.
+the conduct and interpretation of a clinical trial arising from the influence which the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:498: trailing whitespace.
+knowledge of treatment may have on the recruitment and allocation of subjects, their 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:499: trailing whitespace.
+subsequent care, the attitudes of subjects to th e treatments, the assessment of end-points, the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:500: trailing whitespace.
+handling of withdrawals, the exclusion of data from analysis, and so on. The essential aim is 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:501: trailing whitespace.
+to prevent identification of the treatments until all such opportunities for bias have passed. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:502: trailing whitespace.
+A double-blind trial is one in which neither the s ubject nor any of the investigator or sponsor 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:503: trailing whitespace.
+staff who are involved in the treatment or clinical  evaluation of the subjects are aware of the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:504: trailing whitespace.
+treatment received. This includes anyone determining subject eligibility, evaluating endpoints, 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:505: trailing whitespace.
+or assessing compliance with the protocol. This level of blinding is maintained throughout the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:506: trailing whitespace.
+conduct of the trial, and only when the data are cleaned to an acceptable level of quality will 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:507: trailing whitespace.
+appropriate personnel be unblinded. If any of  the sponsor staff who are not involved in the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:508: trailing whitespace.
+treatment or clinical evaluation of the subject s are required to be unblinded to the treatment 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:509: trailing whitespace.
+code (e.g. bioanalytical scientists, auditors, those involved in serious adverse event reporting), 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:510: trailing whitespace.
+the sponsor should have adequate standard operating procedures to guard against 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:511: trailing whitespace.
+inappropriate dissemination of treatment codes. In a single-blind trial the investigator and/or 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:512: trailing whitespace.
+his staff are aware of the treatment but the subjec t is not, or vice versa. In an open-label trial 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:513: trailing whitespace.
+the identity of treatment is known to all. The do uble-blind trial is the optimal approach. This 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:514: trailing whitespace.
+requires that the treatments to be applied during the trial cannot be distinguished (appearance, 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:515: trailing whitespace.
+taste, etc.) either before or during administration, and that the blind is maintained 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:516: trailing whitespace.
+appropriately during the whole trial. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:517: trailing whitespace.
+Difficulties in achieving the double-blind ideal can arise: the treatments may be of a 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:518: trailing whitespace.
+completely different nature, for example, surgery and drug therapy; two drugs may have 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:519: trailing whitespace.
+different formulations and, although they could be made indistinguishable by the use of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:520: trailing whitespace.
+capsules, changing the formulation might also change the pharmacokinetic and/or 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:521: trailing whitespace.
+pharmacodynamic properties and hence require th at bioequivalence of the formulations be 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:522: trailing whitespace.
+established; the daily pattern of administration of two treatments may differ. One way of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:523: trailing whitespace.
+achieving double-blind conditions under these ci rcumstances is to use a 'double-dummy' (see 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:524: trailing whitespace.
+Glossary) technique. This technique may sometimes force an administration scheme that is 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:525: trailing whitespace.
+sufficiently unusual to influence adversely th e motivation and compliance of the subjects. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:526: trailing whitespace.
+Ethical difficulties may also interfere with its use when, for example, it entails dummy 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:527: trailing whitespace.
+operative procedures. Nevertheless, extensive efforts should be made to overcome these 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:528: trailing whitespace.
+difficulties. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:529: trailing whitespace.
+The double-blind nature of some clinical trials  may be partially compromised by apparent 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:530: trailing whitespace.
+treatment induced effects. In such cases, bli nding may be improved by blinding investigators 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:531: trailing whitespace.
+and relevant sponsor staff to certain test result s (e.g. selected clinical  laboratory measures). 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:532: trailing whitespace.
+Similar approaches (see below) to minimising bias in open-label trials should be considered in 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:533: trailing whitespace.
+trials where unique or specific treatment effects may lead to unblinding individual patients. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:534: trailing whitespace.
+If a double-blind trial is not feasible, then the single-blind option should be considered. In 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:536: trailing whitespace.
+label trials provide additional flexibility, but it is particularly important that the investigator's 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:537: trailing whitespace.
+knowledge of the next treatment should not influence the decision to enter the subject; this 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:538: trailing whitespace.
+decision should precede knowledge of the randomised treatment. For these trials, 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:539: trailing whitespace.
+consideration should be given to the use of a centralised randomisation method, such as 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:540: trailing whitespace.
+telephone randomisation, to administer the assign ment of randomised treatment. In addition, 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:541: trailing whitespace.
+clinical assessments should be made by medical staff who are not involved in treating the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:545: trailing whitespace.
+Â© EMEA 2006  12 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:546: trailing whitespace.
+ 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:547: trailing whitespace.
+subjects and who remain blind to treatment. In single-blind or open-label trials every effort 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:548: trailing whitespace.
+should be made to minimise the various known sources of bias and primary variables should 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:549: trailing whitespace.
+be as objective as possible. The reasons for the degree of blinding adopted should be 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:550: trailing whitespace.
+explained in the protocol, together with steps taken to minimise bias by other means. For 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:551: trailing whitespace.
+example, the sponsor should have adequate standard operating procedures to ensure that 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:552: trailing whitespace.
+access to the treatment code is appropriately restricted during the process of cleaning the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:553: trailing whitespace.
+database prior to its release for analysis. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:554: trailing whitespace.
+Breaking the blind (for a single subject) should be considered only when knowledge of the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:555: trailing whitespace.
+treatment assignment is deemed essential by the subjectâ€™s physician for the subjectâ€™s care. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:556: trailing whitespace.
+Any intentional or unintentional breaking of the blind should be reported and explained at the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:557: trailing whitespace.
+end of the trial, irrespective of the reason for its occurrence. The procedure and timing for 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:558: trailing whitespace.
+revealing the treatment assignments should be documented. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:559: trailing whitespace.
+In this document, the blind review (see Glossary ) of data refers to the checking of data during 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:560: trailing whitespace.
+the period of time between trial completion (the  last observation on the last subject) and the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:561: trailing whitespace.
+breaking of the blind. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:562: trailing whitespace.
+2.3.2 Randomisation 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:563: trailing whitespace.
+Randomisation introduces a deliberate element of chance into the assignment of treatments to 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:564: trailing whitespace.
+subjects in a clinical trial. During subsequent analysis of the trial da ta, it provides a sound 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:565: trailing whitespace.
+statistical basis for the quantitative evaluation of the evidence relating to treatment effects. It 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:566: trailing whitespace.
+also tends to produce treatment groups in which the distributions of prognostic factors, known 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:567: trailing whitespace.
+and unknown, are similar. In combination with blinding, randomisation helps to avoid 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:568: trailing whitespace.
+possible bias in the selection and allocation of  subjects arising from the predictability of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:569: trailing whitespace.
+treatment assignments.  
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:570: trailing whitespace.
+The randomisation schedule of a clinical trial documents the random allocation of treatments 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:571: trailing whitespace.
+to subjects. In the simplest situation it is a sequential list of treatments (or treatment sequences 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:572: trailing whitespace.
+in a crossover trial) or corresponding codes by subject number. The logistics of some trials, 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:573: trailing whitespace.
+such as those with a screening phase, may make matters more complicated, but the unique 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:574: trailing whitespace.
+pre-planned assignment of treatment, or tr eatment sequence, to subject should be clear. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:575: trailing whitespace.
+Different trial designs will require differe nt procedures for generating randomisation 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:576: trailing whitespace.
+schedules. The randomisation schedule should be reproducible (if the need arises). 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:577: trailing whitespace.
+Although unrestricted randomisation is an acceptable approach, some advantages can 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:578: trailing whitespace.
+generally be gained by randomising subjects in blocks. This helps to increase the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:579: trailing whitespace.
+comparability of the treatment groups, particul arly when subject characteristics may change 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:580: trailing whitespace.
+over time, as a result, for example, of changes in recruitment policy. It also provides a better 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:581: trailing whitespace.
+guarantee that the treatment groups will be of near ly equal size. In crossover trials it provides 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:582: trailing whitespace.
+the means of obtaining balanced designs with their greater efficiency and easier interpretation. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:583: trailing whitespace.
+Care should be taken to choose block lengths that are sufficiently short to limit possible 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:584: trailing whitespace.
+imbalance, but that are long enough to avoid predictability towards the end of the sequence in 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:585: trailing whitespace.
+a block. Investigators and other relevant staff should generally be blind to the block length; 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:586: trailing whitespace.
+the use of two or more block lengths, randomly selected for each block, can achieve the same 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:587: trailing whitespace.
+purpose. (Theoretically, in a double-blind tr ial predictability does not matter, but the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:588: trailing whitespace.
+pharmacological effects of drugs may provide the opportunity for intelligent guesswork.) 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:589: trailing whitespace.
+In multicentre trials (see Glossary) the ra ndomisation procedures should be organised 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:590: trailing whitespace.
+centrally. It is advisable to have a separate random scheme for each centre, i.e. to stratify by 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:591: trailing whitespace.
+centre or to allocate several whole blocks to each centre. More generally, stratification by 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:592: trailing whitespace.
+important prognostic factors measured at baseline (e .g. severity of disease, age, sex, etc.) may 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:593: trailing whitespace.
+sometimes be valuable in order to promote balanced allocation within strata; this has greater 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:594: trailing whitespace.
+potential benefit in small trials. The use of more  than two or three stratification factors is 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:595: trailing whitespace.
+rarely necessary, is less succe ssful at achieving balance and is logistically troublesome. The 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:599: trailing whitespace.
+Â© EMEA 2006  13 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:600: trailing whitespace.
+ 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:601: trailing whitespace.
+use of a dynamic allocation procedure (see below) may help to achieve balance across a 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:602: trailing whitespace.
+number of stratification factors simultaneously provided the rest of the trial procedures can be 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:603: trailing whitespace.
+adjusted to accommodate an approach of this type. Factors on which randomisation has been 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:604: trailing whitespace.
+stratified should be accounted for later in the analysis. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:605: trailing whitespace.
+The next subject to be randomised into a trial should always receive the treatment 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:606: trailing whitespace.
+corresponding to the next free number in the appropriate randomisation schedule (in the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:607: trailing whitespace.
+respective stratum, if randomi sation is stratified). The appropriate number and associated 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:608: trailing whitespace.
+treatment for the next subject should only be allocated when entry of that subject to the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:609: trailing whitespace.
+randomised part of the trial has been confirmed.  Details of the randomisation that facilitate 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:610: trailing whitespace.
+predictability (e.g. block length) should not  be contained in the trial protocol. The 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:611: trailing whitespace.
+randomisation schedule itself should be filed secu rely by the sponsor or an independent party 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:612: trailing whitespace.
+in a manner that ensures that blindness is properly maintained throughout the trial. Access to 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:613: trailing whitespace.
+the randomisation schedule during the trial should take into account the possibility that, in an 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:614: trailing whitespace.
+emergency, the blind may have to be broken fo r any subject. The procedure to be followed, 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:615: trailing whitespace.
+the necessary documentation, and the subseque nt treatment and assessment of the subject 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:616: trailing whitespace.
+should all be described in the protocol.  
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:617: trailing whitespace.
+Dynamic allocation is an alternative procedur e in which the allocation of treatment to a 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:618: trailing whitespace.
+subject is influenced by the current balance of allo cated treatments and, in a stratified trial, by 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:619: trailing whitespace.
+the stratum to which the subject belongs and th e balance within that stratum. Deterministic 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:620: trailing whitespace.
+dynamic allocation procedures should be avoided and an appropriate element of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:621: trailing whitespace.
+randomisation should be incorporated for each treatment allocation. Ev ery effort should be 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:622: trailing whitespace.
+made to retain the double-blind status of the trial. For example, knowledge of the treatment 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:623: trailing whitespace.
+code may be restricted to a central trial office from where the dynamic allocation is 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:624: trailing whitespace.
+controlled, generally through telephone contact.  This in turn permits additional checks of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:625: trailing whitespace.
+eligibility criteria and establishes entry into the trial, features that can be valuable in certain 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:626: trailing whitespace.
+types of multicentre trial. The usual system of pre-packing and labelling drug supplies for 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:627: trailing whitespace.
+double-blind trials can then be followed, but the order of their use is no longer sequential. It is 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:628: trailing whitespace.
+desirable to use appropriate computer algorithms to keep personnel at the central trial office 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:629: trailing whitespace.
+blind to the treatment code. The complexity of the logistics and potential impact on the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:630: trailing whitespace.
+analysis should be carefully evaluated when considering dynamic allocation. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:631: trailing whitespace.
+III TRIAL DESIGN CONSIDERATIONS 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:632: trailing whitespace.
+3.1 Design Configuration 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:633: trailing whitespace.
+3.1.1 Parallel Group Design 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:634: trailing whitespace.
+The most common clinical trial design for confirmatory trials is the parallel group design in 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:635: trailing whitespace.
+which subjects are randomised to one of two or more arms, each arm being allocated a 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:636: trailing whitespace.
+different treatment. These treatments will incl ude the investigational product at one or more 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:637: trailing whitespace.
+doses, and one or more control treatments, such as placebo and/or an active comparator. The 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:638: trailing whitespace.
+assumptions underlying this design are less complex than for most other designs. However, as 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:639: trailing whitespace.
+with other designs, there may be additional featur es of the trial that complicate the analysis 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:640: trailing whitespace.
+and interpretation (e.g. covariates, repeated me asurements over time, interactions between 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:641: trailing whitespace.
+design factors, protocol violations, dropouts (see Glossary) and withdrawals). 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:642: trailing whitespace.
+3.1.2 Crossover Design 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:643: trailing whitespace.
+In the crossover design, each subject is randomised to a sequence of two or more treatments, 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:644: trailing whitespace.
+and hence acts as his own control for treatment comparisons. This simple manoeuvre is 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:645: trailing whitespace.
+attractive primarily because it reduces the number of subjects and usually the number of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:649: trailing whitespace.
+Â© EMEA 2006  14 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:650: trailing whitespace.
+ 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:651: trailing whitespace.
+assessments needed to achieve a specific power, sometimes to a marked extent. In the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:652: trailing whitespace.
+simplest 2Ã—2 crossover design each subject recei ves each of two treatments in randomised 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:653: trailing whitespace.
+order in two successive treatment periods, of ten separated by a washout period. The most 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:654: trailing whitespace.
+common extension of this entails comparing n(>2) treatments in n periods, each subject 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:655: trailing whitespace.
+receiving all n treatments. Numerous variations exist, such as designs in which each subject 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:656: trailing whitespace.
+receives a subset of n(>2) treatments, or ones in which treatments are repeated within a 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:657: trailing whitespace.
+subject. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:658: trailing whitespace.
+Crossover designs have a number of problems th at can invalidate their results. The chief 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:659: trailing whitespace.
+difficulty concerns carryover, that is, the re sidual influence of treatments in subsequent 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:660: trailing whitespace.
+treatment periods. In an additive model the effect  of unequal carryover will be to bias direct 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:661: trailing whitespace.
+treatment comparisons. In the 2Ã—2 design the carryover effect cannot be statistically 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:662: trailing whitespace.
+distinguished from the interaction between treatment and period and the test for either of these 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:663: trailing whitespace.
+effects lacks power because the corresponding contra st is 'between subject'. This problem is 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:664: trailing whitespace.
+less acute in higher order designs, but cannot be entirely dismissed. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:665: trailing whitespace.
+When the crossover design is used it is theref ore important to avoid carryover. This is best 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:666: trailing whitespace.
+done by selective and careful use of the design on the basis of adequate knowledge of both the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:667: trailing whitespace.
+disease area and the new medication. The dise ase under study should be chronic and stable. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:668: trailing whitespace.
+The relevant effects of the medication should develop fully within the treatment period. The 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:669: trailing whitespace.
+washout periods should be sufficiently long for co mplete reversibility of drug effect. The fact 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:670: trailing whitespace.
+that these conditions are likely to be met shoul d be established in advance of the trial by 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:671: trailing whitespace.
+means of prior information and data. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:672: trailing whitespace.
+There are additional problems that need careful attention in crossover trials. The most notable 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:673: trailing whitespace.
+of these are the complications of analysis and interpretation arising from the loss of subjects. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:674: trailing whitespace.
+Also, the potential for carryover leads to difficulties in assigning adverse events which occur 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:675: trailing whitespace.
+in later treatment periods to the appropriate treatment. These, and other issues, are described 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:676: trailing whitespace.
+in ICH E4. The crossover design should generally be restricted to situat ions where losses of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:677: trailing whitespace.
+subjects from the trial are expected to be small. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:678: trailing whitespace.
+A common, and generally satisfactory, use of th e 2Ã—2 crossover design is to demonstrate the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:679: trailing whitespace.
+bioequivalence of two formulations of the same medication. In this particular application in 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:680: trailing whitespace.
+healthy volunteers, carryover effects on the relevant pharmacokinetic variable are most 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:681: trailing whitespace.
+unlikely to occur if the wash-out time between the two periods is sufficiently long. However it 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:682: trailing whitespace.
+is still important to check this assumption during analysis on the basis of the data obtained, 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:683: trailing whitespace.
+for example by demonstrating that no drug is detectable at the start of each period. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:684: trailing whitespace.
+3.1.3 Factorial Designs 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:685: trailing whitespace.
+In a factorial design two or more treatments are evaluated simultaneously through the use of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:686: trailing whitespace.
+varying combinations of the treatments. The simplest example is the 2Ã—2 factorial design in 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:687: trailing whitespace.
+which subjects are randomly allocated to one of the four possible combinations of two 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:688: trailing whitespace.
+treatments, A and B say. These are: A alone; B alone; both A and B; neither A nor B. In many 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:689: trailing whitespace.
+cases this design is used for the specific purpose of examining the interaction of A and B. The 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:690: trailing whitespace.
+statistical test of interaction may lack power to  detect an interaction if the sample size was 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:691: trailing whitespace.
+calculated based on the test for main effects. This consideration is important when this design 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:692: trailing whitespace.
+is used for examining the joint effects of A and B, in particular, if the treatments are likely to 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:693: trailing whitespace.
+be used together. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:694: trailing whitespace.
+Another important use of the factorial design is  to establish the dose-response characteristics 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:695: trailing whitespace.
+of the simultaneous use of treatments C and D, especially when the efficacy of each 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:696: trailing whitespace.
+monotherapy has been established at some dose in prior trials. A number, m, of doses of C is 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:697: trailing whitespace.
+selected, usually including a zero dose (placebo), and a similar number, n, of doses of D. The 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:698: trailing whitespace.
+full design then consists of mÃ—n treatment gr oups, each receiving a different combination of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:702: trailing whitespace.
+Â© EMEA 2006  15 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:703: trailing whitespace.
+ 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:704: trailing whitespace.
+doses of C and D. The resulting estimate of the response surface may then be used to help to 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:705: trailing whitespace.
+identify an appropriate combination of doses of C and D for clinical use (see ICH E4). 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:706: trailing whitespace.
+In some cases, the 2Ã—2 design may be used to ma ke efficient use of clinical trial subjects by 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:707: trailing whitespace.
+evaluating the efficacy of the two treatments with the same number of subjects as would be 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:708: trailing whitespace.
+required to evaluate the efficacy of either one alone. This strategy has proved to be 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:709: trailing whitespace.
+particularly valuable for very large mortality trials. The efficiency and validity of this 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:710: trailing whitespace.
+approach depends upon the absence of interaction between treatments A and B so that the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:711: trailing whitespace.
+effects of A and B on the primary efficacy vari ables follow an additive model, and hence the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:712: trailing whitespace.
+effect of A is virtually identical whether or not it is additional to the effect of B. As for the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:713: trailing whitespace.
+crossover trial, evidence that this condition is likely to be met should be established in 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:714: trailing whitespace.
+advance of the trial by means of prior information and data.  
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:715: trailing whitespace.
+3.2 Multicentre Trials  
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:716: trailing whitespace.
+Multicentre trials are carried out for two main reasons. Firstly, a multicentre trial is an 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:717: trailing whitespace.
+accepted way of evaluating a new medication more efficiently; under some circumstances, it 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:718: trailing whitespace.
+may present the only practical means of accrui ng sufficient subjects to satisfy the trial 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:719: trailing whitespace.
+objective within a reasonable time-frame. Multicentre trials of this nature may, in principle, 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:720: trailing whitespace.
+be carried out at any stage of clinical development. They may have several centres with a 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:721: trailing whitespace.
+large number of subjects per centre or, in the case of a rare disease, they may have a large 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:722: trailing whitespace.
+number of centres with very few subjects per centre. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:723: trailing whitespace.
+Secondly, a trial may be designed as a multicen tre (and multi-investigator) trial primarily to 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:724: trailing whitespace.
+provide a better basis for the subsequent genera lisation of its findings. This arises from the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:725: trailing whitespace.
+possibility of recruiting the subjects from a wider population and of administering the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:726: trailing whitespace.
+medication in a broader range of clinical settings, thus presenting an experimental situation 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:727: trailing whitespace.
+that is more typical of future use. In this case the involvement of a number of investigators 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:728: trailing whitespace.
+also gives the potential for a wider range of clinical judgement concerning the value of the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:729: trailing whitespace.
+medication. Such a trial would be a confirmatory trial in the later phases of drug development 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:730: trailing whitespace.
+and would be likely to involve a large number of investigators and centres. It might 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:731: trailing whitespace.
+sometimes be conducted in a number of di fferent countries in order to facilitate 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:732: trailing whitespace.
+generalisability (see Glossary) even further. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:733: trailing whitespace.
+If a multicentre trial is to be meaningfully interpreted and extrapolated, then the manner in 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:734: trailing whitespace.
+which the protocol is implemented should be clear and similar at all centres. Furthermore the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:735: trailing whitespace.
+usual sample size and power calculations depend upon the assumption that the differences 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:736: trailing whitespace.
+between the compared treatments in the centres are unbiased estimates of the same quantity. It 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:737: trailing whitespace.
+is important to design the common protocol and to conduct the trial with this background in 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:738: trailing whitespace.
+mind. Procedures should be standardised as co mpletely as possible. Variation of evaluation 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:739: trailing whitespace.
+criteria and schemes can be reduced by investig ator meetings, by the training of personnel in 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:740: trailing whitespace.
+advance of the trial and by careful monitori ng during the trial. Good design should generally 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:741: trailing whitespace.
+aim to achieve the same distribution of subj ects to treatments within each centre and good 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:742: trailing whitespace.
+management should maintain this design objective. Trials that avoid excessive variation in the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:743: trailing whitespace.
+numbers of subjects per centre and trials that avoid a few very small centres have advantages 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:744: trailing whitespace.
+if it is later found necessary to take into account the heterogeneity of the treatment effect from 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:745: trailing whitespace.
+centre to centre, because they reduce the differe nces between different weighted estimates of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:746: trailing whitespace.
+the treatment effect. (This point does not apply to trials in which all centres are very small and 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:747: trailing whitespace.
+in which centre does not feature in the analysis.) Failure to take these precautions, combined 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:748: trailing whitespace.
+with doubts about the homogeneity of the results may, in severe cases, reduce the value of a 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:749: trailing whitespace.
+multicentre trial to such a degree that it cannot be regarded as giving convincing evidence for 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:750: trailing whitespace.
+the sponsorâ€™s claims. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:751: trailing whitespace.
+In the simplest multicentre trial, each inves tigator will be responsible for the subjects 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:752: trailing whitespace.
+recruited at one hospital, so that  â€˜centreâ€™ is identified uniquely by either investigator or 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:756: trailing whitespace.
+Â© EMEA 2006  16 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:757: trailing whitespace.
+ 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:758: trailing whitespace.
+hospital. In many trials, however, the situation is more complex. One investigator may recruit 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:759: trailing whitespace.
+subjects from several hospitals; one investig ator may represent a team of clinicians 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:760: trailing whitespace.
+(subinvestigators) who all recruit subjects from th eir own clinics at one hospital or at several 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:761: trailing whitespace.
+associated hospitals. Whenever  there is room for doubt about the definition of centre in a 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:762: trailing whitespace.
+statistical model, the statistical section of the protocol (see Section 5.1) should clearly define 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:763: trailing whitespace.
+the term (e.g. by investigator, location or region) in the context of the particular trial. In most 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:764: trailing whitespace.
+instances centres can be satisfactorily define d through the investigators and ICH E6 provides 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:765: trailing whitespace.
+relevant guidance in this respect. In cases of doubt the aim should be to define centres so as to 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:766: trailing whitespace.
+achieve homogeneity in the important factors affecting the measurements of the primary 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:767: trailing whitespace.
+variables and the influence of the treatments. Any rules for combining centres in the analysis 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:768: trailing whitespace.
+should be justified and specified prospectively in the protocol where possible, but in any case 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:769: trailing whitespace.
+decisions concerning this approach should always be taken blind to treatment, for example at 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:770: trailing whitespace.
+the time of the blind review. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:771: trailing whitespace.
+The statistical model to be adopted for the es timation and testing of treatment effects should 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:772: trailing whitespace.
+be described in the protocol. The main treatment effect may be investigated first using a 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:773: trailing whitespace.
+model which allows for centre differences, but does not include a term for treatment-by-centre 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:774: trailing whitespace.
+interaction. If the treatment effect is homogeneous across centres, the routine inclusion of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:775: trailing whitespace.
+interaction terms in the model reduces the efficiency of the test for the main effects. In the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:776: trailing whitespace.
+presence of true heterogeneity of treatment eff ects, the interpretation of the main treatment 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:777: trailing whitespace.
+effect is controversial. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:778: trailing whitespace.
+In some trials, for example some large mortality trials with very few subjects per centre, there 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:779: trailing whitespace.
+may be no reason to expect the centres to have any influence on the primary or secondary 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:780: trailing whitespace.
+variables because they are unlikely to represent influences of clinical importance. In other 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:781: trailing whitespace.
+trials it may be recognised from the start that the limited numbers of subjects per centre will 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:782: trailing whitespace.
+make it impracticable to include the centre effects in the statistical model. In these cases it is 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:783: trailing whitespace.
+not appropriate to include a term for centre in the model, and it is not necessary to stratify the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:784: trailing whitespace.
+randomisation by centre in this situation. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:785: trailing whitespace.
+If positive treatment effects are found in a tr ial with appreciable numbers of subjects per 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:786: trailing whitespace.
+centre, there should generally be an exploratio n of the heterogeneity of treatment effects 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:787: trailing whitespace.
+across centres, as this may affect the generalisability of the conclusions. Marked 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:788: trailing whitespace.
+heterogeneity may be identified by graphical disp lay of the results of individual centres or by 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:789: trailing whitespace.
+analytical methods, such as a significance test  of the treatment-by-centre interaction. When 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:790: trailing whitespace.
+using such a statistical significance test, it is important to recognise that this generally has low 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:791: trailing whitespace.
+power in a trial designed to detect the main effect of treatment. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:792: trailing whitespace.
+If heterogeneity of treatment effects is found, this should be interpreted with care and 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:793: trailing whitespace.
+vigorous attempts should be made to find an expl anation in terms of other features of trial 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:794: trailing whitespace.
+management or subject characteristics. Such an  explanation will usua lly suggest appropriate 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:795: trailing whitespace.
+further analysis and interpreta tion. In the absence of an explanation, heterogeneity of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:796: trailing whitespace.
+treatment effect as evidenced, for example, by marked quantitative interactions (see Glossary) 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:797: trailing whitespace.
+implies that alternative estimates of the trea tment effect may be required, giving different 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:798: trailing whitespace.
+weights to the centres, in order to substantia te the robustness of the estimates of treatment 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:799: trailing whitespace.
+effect. It is even more important to understand the basis of any heterogeneity characterised by 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:800: trailing whitespace.
+marked qualitative interactions (see Glossary ), and failure to find an explanation may 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:801: trailing whitespace.
+necessitate further clinical trials before the treatment effect can be reliably predicted. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:802: trailing whitespace.
+Up to this point the discussion of multicentre tr ials has been based on the use of fixed effect 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:803: trailing whitespace.
+models. Mixed models may also be used to expl ore the heterogeneity of the treatment effect. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:804: trailing whitespace.
+These models consider centre and treatment-by-centre effects to be random, and are 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:805: trailing whitespace.
+especially relevant when the number of sites is large. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:809: trailing whitespace.
+Â© EMEA 2006  17 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:810: trailing whitespace.
+ 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:811: trailing whitespace.
+3.3 Type of Comparison 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:812: trailing whitespace.
+3.3.1 Trials to Show Superiority 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:813: trailing whitespace.
+Scientifically, efficacy is most convincingly established by demonstrating superiority to 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:814: trailing whitespace.
+placebo in a placebo-controlled trial, by showi ng superiority to an active control treatment or 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:815: trailing whitespace.
+by demonstrating a dose-response relationship. This type of trial is referred to as a 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:816: trailing whitespace.
+â€˜superiorityâ€™ trial (see Glossary). Generally in  this guidance superiority trials are assumed, 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:817: trailing whitespace.
+unless it is explicitly stated otherwise. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:818: trailing whitespace.
+For serious illnesses, when a therapeutic treatment which has been shown to be efficacious by 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:819: trailing whitespace.
+superiority trial(s) exists, a placebo-controlled trial may be considered unethical. In that case 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:820: trailing whitespace.
+the scientifically sound use of an active trea tment as a control should be considered. The 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:821: trailing whitespace.
+appropriateness of placebo control vs. active co ntrol should be considered on a trial by trial 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:822: trailing whitespace.
+basis. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:823: trailing whitespace.
+3.3.2 Trials to Show Equivalence or Non-inferiority 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:824: trailing whitespace.
+In some cases, an investigational product is compared to a reference treatment without the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:825: trailing whitespace.
+objective of showing superiority. This type of trial is divided into two major categories 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:827: trailing whitespace.
+inferiority' trial (see Glossary). 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:828: trailing whitespace.
+Bioequivalence trials fall into the former category. In some situations, clinical equivalence 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:829: trailing whitespace.
+trials are also undertaken for other regulatory reasons such as demonstrating the clinical 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:830: trailing whitespace.
+equivalence of a generic product to the marketed product when the compound is not absorbed 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:831: trailing whitespace.
+and therefore not present in the blood stream. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:832: trailing whitespace.
+Many active control trials are designed to show that the efficacy of an investigational product 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:833: trailing whitespace.
+is no worse than that of the active comparator, and hence fall into the latter category. Another 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:834: trailing whitespace.
+possibility is a trial in which multiple doses of the investigational drug are compared with the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:835: trailing whitespace.
+recommended dose or multiple doses of the st andard drug. The purpose of this design is 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:836: trailing whitespace.
+simultaneously to show a dose-response relationship for the investigational product and to 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:837: trailing whitespace.
+compare the investigational product with the active control.  
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:838: trailing whitespace.
+Active control equivalence or non-inferiority trials may also incorporate a placebo, thus 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:839: trailing whitespace.
+pursuing multiple goals in one trial; for example, they may establish superiority to placebo 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:840: trailing whitespace.
+and hence validate the trial design and simultaneously evaluate the degree of similarity of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:841: trailing whitespace.
+efficacy and safety to the active comparator. There are well known difficulties associated with 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:842: trailing whitespace.
+the use of the active control equivalence (or non-inferiority) trials that do not incorporate a 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:843: trailing whitespace.
+placebo or do not use multiple doses of the new dr ug. These relate to the implicit lack of any 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:844: trailing whitespace.
+measure of internal validity (in contrast to supe riority trials), thus making external validation 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:845: trailing whitespace.
+necessary. The equivalence (or non-inferiority) trial is not conservative in nature, so that 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:846: trailing whitespace.
+many flaws in the design or conduct of the trial will tend to bias the results towards a 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:847: trailing whitespace.
+conclusion of equivalence. For these reasons, th e design features of such trials should receive 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:848: trailing whitespace.
+special attention and their conduct needs special care. For example, it is especially important 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:849: trailing whitespace.
+to minimise the incidence of violations of the entry criteria, non-compliance, withdrawals, 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:850: trailing whitespace.
+losses to follow-up, missing data and other devi ations from the protocol, and also to minimise 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:851: trailing whitespace.
+their impact on the subsequent analyses. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:852: trailing whitespace.
+Active comparators should be chosen with care. An example of a suitable active comparator 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:853: trailing whitespace.
+would be a widely used therapy whose efficacy in the relevant indication has been clearly 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:854: trailing whitespace.
+established and quantified in well designed and well documented superiority trial(s) and 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:855: trailing whitespace.
+which can be reliably expected to exhibit similar efficacy in the contemplated active control 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:856: trailing whitespace.
+trial. To this end, the new trial should have  the same important design features (primary 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:857: trailing whitespace.
+variables, the dose of the active comparator, eligibility criteria, etc.) as the previously 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:861: trailing whitespace.
+Â© EMEA 2006  18 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:862: trailing whitespace.
+ 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:863: trailing whitespace.
+conducted superiority trials in which the activ e comparator clearly demonstrated clinically 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:864: trailing whitespace.
+relevant efficacy, taking into account advances in medical or statistical practice relevant to the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:865: trailing whitespace.
+new trial. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:866: trailing whitespace.
+It is vital that the protocol of a trial designed to demonstrate equivalence or non-inferiority 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:867: trailing whitespace.
+contain a clear statement that this is its explic it intention. An equivalence margin should be 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:868: trailing whitespace.
+specified in the protocol; this margin is the largest difference that can be judged as being 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:869: trailing whitespace.
+clinically acceptable and should be smaller than differences observed in superiority trials of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:870: trailing whitespace.
+the active comparator. For the active control equivalence trial, both the upper and the lower 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:871: trailing whitespace.
+equivalence margins are needed, while only the lower margin is needed for the active control 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:872: trailing whitespace.
+non-inferiority trial. The choice of equivalence margins should be justified clinically. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:873: trailing whitespace.
+Statistical analysis is generally based on the use of confidence intervals (see Section 5.5). For 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:874: trailing whitespace.
+equivalence trials, two-sided confidence intervals should be used. Equivalence is inferred 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:875: trailing whitespace.
+when the entire confidence interval falls within the equivalence margins. Operationally, this is 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:876: trailing whitespace.
+equivalent to the method of using two simultaneous one-sided tests to test the (composite) 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:877: trailing whitespace.
+null hypothesis that the treatment difference is outside the equivalence margins versus the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:878: trailing whitespace.
+(composite) alternative hypothesis that the tr eatment difference is within the margins. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:879: trailing whitespace.
+Because the two null hypotheses are disjoint, the type I error is appropriately controlled. For 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:880: trailing whitespace.
+non-inferiority trials a one-sided interval shoul d be used. The confidence interval approach 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:881: trailing whitespace.
+has a one-sided hypothesis test counterpart for testing the null hypothesis that the treatment 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:882: trailing whitespace.
+difference (investigational product minus control) is equal to the lower equivalence margin 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:883: trailing whitespace.
+versus the alternative that the treatment difference is greater than the lower equivalence 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:885: trailing whitespace.
+sided or two-sided procedure. Sample size calculations should be based on these methods (see 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:886: trailing whitespace.
+Section 3.5). 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:887: trailing whitespace.
+Concluding equivalence or non-inferiority base d on observing a non-significant test result of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:888: trailing whitespace.
+the null hypothesis that there is no difference between the investigational product and the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:889: trailing whitespace.
+active comparator is inappropriate. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:890: trailing whitespace.
+There are also special issues in  the choice of analysis sets. S ubjects who withdraw or dropout 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:891: trailing whitespace.
+of the treatment group or the comparator group will tend to have a lack of response, and hence 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:892: trailing whitespace.
+the results of using the full analysis set (see Glossary) may be biased toward demonstrating 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:893: trailing whitespace.
+equivalence (see Section 5.2.3). 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:894: trailing whitespace.
+3.3.3 Trials to Show Dose-response Relationship 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:895: trailing whitespace.
+How response is related to the dose of a new investigational product is a question to which 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:896: trailing whitespace.
+answers may be obtained in all phases of development, and by a variety of approaches (see 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:897: trailing whitespace.
+ICH E4). Dose-response trials may serve a number of objectives, amongst which the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:898: trailing whitespace.
+following are of particular importance: the confirmation of efficacy; the investigation of the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:899: trailing whitespace.
+shape and location of the dose-response curve; the estimation of an appropriate starting dose; 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:900: trailing whitespace.
+the identification of optimal strategies for indi vidual dose adjustments; the determination of a 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:901: trailing whitespace.
+maximal dose beyond which additional benefit would be unlikely to occur. These objectives 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:902: trailing whitespace.
+should be addressed using the data collecte d at a number of doses under investigation, 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:903: trailing whitespace.
+including a placebo (zero dose) wherever appr opriate. For this purpose the application of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:904: trailing whitespace.
+procedures to estimate the relationship betw een dose and response, including the construction 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:905: trailing whitespace.
+of confidence intervals and the use of graphi cal methods, is as important as the use of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:906: trailing whitespace.
+statistical tests. The hypothesis tests that are used may need to be tailored to the natural 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:907: trailing whitespace.
+ordering of doses or to particular questions regarding the shape of the dose-response curve 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:908: trailing whitespace.
+(e.g. monotonicity). The details of the planned statistical procedures should be given in the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:909: trailing whitespace.
+protocol. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:913: trailing whitespace.
+Â© EMEA 2006  19 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:914: trailing whitespace.
+ 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:915: trailing whitespace.
+3.4 Group Sequential Designs 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:916: trailing whitespace.
+Group sequential designs are used to facilitate th e conduct of interim analysis (see section 4.5 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:917: trailing whitespace.
+and Glossary). While group sequential designs are not the only acceptable types of designs 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:918: trailing whitespace.
+permitting interim analysis, they are the most commonly applied because it is more 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:919: trailing whitespace.
+practicable to assess grouped subject outcomes at  periodic intervals during the trial than on a 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:920: trailing whitespace.
+continuous basis as data from each subject become available. The statistical methods should 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:921: trailing whitespace.
+be fully specified in advance of the availability of information on treatment outcomes and 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:922: trailing whitespace.
+subject treatment assignments (i.e. blind brea king, see Section 4.5). An Independent Data 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:923: trailing whitespace.
+Monitoring Committee (see Glossary) may be us ed to review or to conduct the interim 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:924: trailing whitespace.
+analysis of data arising from a group sequential design (see Section 4.6). While the design has 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:926: trailing whitespace.
+fatal endpoints, its use is growing in other circum stances. In particular, it is recognised that 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:927: trailing whitespace.
+safety must be monitored in all trials and ther efore the need for formal procedures to cover 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:928: trailing whitespace.
+early stopping for safety reasons should always be considered. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:929: trailing whitespace.
+3.5 Sample Size 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:930: trailing whitespace.
+The number of subjects in a clinical trial shou ld always be large enough to provide a reliable 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:931: trailing whitespace.
+answer to the questions addressed. This nu mber is usually determined by the primary 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:932: trailing whitespace.
+objective of the trial. If the sample size is dete rmined on some other basis, then this should be 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:933: trailing whitespace.
+made clear and justified. For example, a trial sized on the basis of safety questions or 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:934: trailing whitespace.
+requirements or important secondary objectives may need larger numbers of subjects than a 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:935: trailing whitespace.
+trial sized on the basis of the primary efficacy question (see, for example, ICH E1a). 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:936: trailing whitespace.
+Using the usual method for determining the appropriate sample size, the following items 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:937: trailing whitespace.
+should be specified: a primary variable, the test statistic, the null hypothesis, the alternative 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:938: trailing whitespace.
+('working') hypothesis at the chosen dose(s) (embodying consideration of the treatment 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:939: trailing whitespace.
+difference to be detected or rejected at the dose and in the subject population selected), the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:940: trailing whitespace.
+probability of erroneously rejecting the null hypothesis (the type I error), and the probability 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:941: trailing whitespace.
+of erroneously failing to reject the null hypothes is (the type II error), as well as the approach 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:942: trailing whitespace.
+to dealing with treatment withdrawals and protocol violations. In some instances, the event 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:943: trailing whitespace.
+rate is of primary interest for evaluating power, and assumptions should be made to 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:944: trailing whitespace.
+extrapolate from the required number of events to the eventual sample size for the trial. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:945: trailing whitespace.
+The method by which the sample size is calculated should be given in the protocol, together 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:946: trailing whitespace.
+with the estimates of any quantities used in the calculations (such as variances, mean values, 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:947: trailing whitespace.
+response rates, event rates, difference to be detected). The basis of these estimates should also 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:948: trailing whitespace.
+be given. It is important to investigate the sensitivity of the sample size estimate to a variety 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:949: trailing whitespace.
+of deviations from these assumptions and this may be facilitated by providing a range of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:950: trailing whitespace.
+sample sizes appropriate for a reasonable range of deviations from assumptions. In 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:951: trailing whitespace.
+confirmatory trials, assumptions should normally be based on published data or on the results 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:952: trailing whitespace.
+of earlier trials. The treatment difference to be detected may be based on a judgement 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:953: trailing whitespace.
+concerning the minimal effect which has clinical relevance in the management of patients or 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:954: trailing whitespace.
+on a judgement concerning the anticipated effect of the new treatment, where this is larger. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:955: trailing whitespace.
+Conventionally the probability of type I error is set at 5% or less or as dictated by any 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:956: trailing whitespace.
+adjustments made necessary for multiplicity considerations; the precise choice may be 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:957: trailing whitespace.
+influenced by the prior plausibility of the hypothesis under test and the desired impact of the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:958: trailing whitespace.
+results. The probability of type II error is conventionally set at 10% to 20%; it is in the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:959: trailing whitespace.
+sponsorâ€™s interest to keep this figure as low as feasible especially in the case of trials that are 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:960: trailing whitespace.
+difficult or impossible to repeat. Alternative va lues to the conventional levels of type I and 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:961: trailing whitespace.
+type II error may be acceptable or even preferable in some cases. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:962: trailing whitespace.
+Sample size calculations should refer to the number of subjects required for the primary 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:963: trailing whitespace.
+analysis. If this is the 'full analysis set', estimates of the effect size may need to be reduced 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:967: trailing whitespace.
+Â© EMEA 2006  20 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:968: trailing whitespace.
+ 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:969: trailing whitespace.
+compared to the per protocol set (see Glossary ). This is to allow for the dilution of the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:970: trailing whitespace.
+treatment effect arising from the inclusion of data from patients who have withdrawn from 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:971: trailing whitespace.
+treatment or whose compliance is poor. The assumptions about variability may also need to 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:972: trailing whitespace.
+be revised. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:973: trailing whitespace.
+The sample size of an equivalence trial or a non-inferiority trial (see  Section 3.3.2) should 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:974: trailing whitespace.
+normally be based on the objective of obtaining a confidence interval for the treatment 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:975: trailing whitespace.
+difference that shows that the treatments differ at most by a clinically acceptable difference. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:976: trailing whitespace.
+When the power of an equivalence trial is assessed at a true difference of zero, then the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:977: trailing whitespace.
+sample size necessary to achieve this power is  underestimated if the true difference is not 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:978: trailing whitespace.
+zero. When the power of a non-inferiority trial is assessed at a zero difference, then the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:979: trailing whitespace.
+sample size needed to achieve that power wi ll be underestimated if the effect of the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:980: trailing whitespace.
+investigational product is less than that of the active control. The choice of a 'clinically 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:981: trailing whitespace.
+acceptableâ€™ difference needs justification with respect to its meaning for future patients, and 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:982: trailing whitespace.
+may be smaller than the 'clinically relevant' difference referred to a bove in the context of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:983: trailing whitespace.
+superiority trials designed to establish that a difference exists.  
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:984: trailing whitespace.
+The exact sample size in a group sequential trial cannot be fixed in advance because it 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:985: trailing whitespace.
+depends upon the play of chance in combination with the chosen stopping guideline and the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:986: trailing whitespace.
+true treatment difference. The design of the stopping guideline should take into account the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:987: trailing whitespace.
+consequent distribution of the sample size, usually embodied in the expected and maximum 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:988: trailing whitespace.
+sample sizes. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:989: trailing whitespace.
+When event rates are lower than anticipated or va riability is larger than expected, methods for 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:990: trailing whitespace.
+sample size re-estimation are available without unblinding data or making treatment 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:991: trailing whitespace.
+comparisons (see Section 4.4). 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:992: trailing whitespace.
+3.6 Data Capture and Processing 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:993: trailing whitespace.
+The collection of data and transfer of data from  the investigator to the sponsor can take place 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:994: trailing whitespace.
+through a variety of media, including paper ca se record forms, remote site monitoring 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:995: trailing whitespace.
+systems, medical computer systems and electronic transfer. Whatever data capture instrument 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:996: trailing whitespace.
+is used, the form and content of the information collected should be in full accordance with 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:997: trailing whitespace.
+the protocol and should be established in advance of the conduct of the clinical trial. It should 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:998: trailing whitespace.
+focus on the data necessary to implement the planned analysis, including the context 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:999: trailing whitespace.
+information (such as timing assessments relativ e to dosing) necessary to confirm protocol 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1000: trailing whitespace.
+compliance or identify important protocol  deviations. â€˜Missing valuesâ€™ should be 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1001: trailing whitespace.
+distinguishable from the â€˜value zeroâ€™ or â€˜characteristic absentâ€™. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1002: trailing whitespace.
+The process of data capture through to databa se finalisation should be carried out in 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1003: trailing whitespace.
+accordance with GCP (see ICH E6, Section 5). Specifically, timely and reliable processes for 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1004: trailing whitespace.
+recording data and rectifying errors and omissi ons are necessary to ensure delivery of a 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1005: trailing whitespace.
+quality database and the achievement of the trial objectives through the implementation of the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1006: trailing whitespace.
+planned analysis. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1007: trailing whitespace.
+IV TRIAL CONDUCT CONSIDERATIONS 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1008: trailing whitespace.
+4.1 Trial Monitoring and Interim Analysis 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1009: trailing whitespace.
+Careful conduct of a clinical trial according to the protocol has a major impact on the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1010: trailing whitespace.
+credibility of the results (see ICH E6). Carefu l monitoring can ensure that difficulties are 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1011: trailing whitespace.
+noticed early and their occurrence or recurrence minimised. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1015: trailing whitespace.
+Â© EMEA 2006  21 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1016: trailing whitespace.
+ 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1017: trailing whitespace.
+There are two distinct types of monitoring that generally characterise confirmatory clinical 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1018: trailing whitespace.
+trials sponsored by the pharmaceutical industry. One type of monitoring concerns the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1019: trailing whitespace.
+oversight of the quality of the trial, while the other type involves breaking the blind to make 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1020: trailing whitespace.
+treatment comparisons (i.e. interim analysis). Both types of trial monitoring, in addition to 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1021: trailing whitespace.
+entailing different staff responsibilities, involv e access to different types of trial data and 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1022: trailing whitespace.
+information, and thus different principles appl y for the control of potential statistical and 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1023: trailing whitespace.
+operational bias. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1024: trailing whitespace.
+For the purpose of overseeing the quality of the trial the checks involved in trial monitoring 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1025: trailing whitespace.
+may include whether the protocol is being followed, the acceptability of data being accrued, 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1026: trailing whitespace.
+the success of planned accrual targets, the appropriateness of the design assumptions, success 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1027: trailing whitespace.
+in keeping patients in the trials, etc. (see Sections 4.2 to 4.4). This type of monitoring does not 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1028: trailing whitespace.
+require access to information on comparative tr eatment effects, nor unblinding of data and 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1029: trailing whitespace.
+therefore has no impact on type I error. The monitoring of a trial for this purpose is the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1030: trailing whitespace.
+responsibility of the sponsor (see ICH E6) a nd can be carried out by the sponsor or an 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1031: trailing whitespace.
+independent group selected by the sponsor. The period for this type of monitoring usually 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1032: trailing whitespace.
+starts with the selection of the trial sites and ends with the collection and cleaning of the last 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1033: trailing whitespace.
+subjectâ€™s data. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1034: trailing whitespace.
+The other type of trial monitoring (interim analysis) involves the accruing of comparative 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1035: trailing whitespace.
+treatment results. Interim analysis requires unbl inded (i.e. key breaking) access to treatment 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1036: trailing whitespace.
+group assignment (actual treatment assignment or identification of group assignment) and 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1037: trailing whitespace.
+comparative treatment group summary information.  This necessitates that the protocol (or 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1038: trailing whitespace.
+appropriate amendments prior to a first analysis) contains statistical plans for the interim 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1039: trailing whitespace.
+analysis to prevent certain types of bias. This is discussed in Sections 4.5 & 4.6. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1040: trailing whitespace.
+4.2 Changes in Inclusion and Exclusion Criteria 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1041: trailing whitespace.
+Inclusion and exclusion criteria should remain  constant, as specified in the protocol, 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1042: trailing whitespace.
+throughout the period of subject recruitment. Changes may occasionally be appropriate, for 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1043: trailing whitespace.
+example, in long term trials, where growing medical knowledge either from outside the trial 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1044: trailing whitespace.
+or from interim analyses may suggest a change of entry criteria. Changes may also result from 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1045: trailing whitespace.
+the discovery by monitoring staff that regular viol ations of the entry criteria are occurring, or 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1046: trailing whitespace.
+that seriously low recruitment rates are due to over-restrictive criteria. Changes should be 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1047: trailing whitespace.
+made without breaking the blind and should always be described by a protocol amendment 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1048: trailing whitespace.
+which should cover any statistical consequences, such as sample size adjustments arising 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1049: trailing whitespace.
+from different event rates, or mo difications to the planned analysis, such as stratifying the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1050: trailing whitespace.
+analysis according to modified inclusion/exclusion criteria. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1051: trailing whitespace.
+4.3 Accrual Rates 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1052: trailing whitespace.
+In trials with a long time-scale for the accrua l of subjects, the rate of accrual should be 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1053: trailing whitespace.
+monitored and, if it falls appreciably below the projected level, the reasons should be 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1054: trailing whitespace.
+identified and remedial actions taken in order to protect the power of the trial and alleviate 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1055: trailing whitespace.
+concerns about selective entry and other aspe cts of quality. In a multicentre trial these 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1056: trailing whitespace.
+considerations apply to the individual centres. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1057: trailing whitespace.
+4.4 Sample Size Adjustment 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1058: trailing whitespace.
+In long term trials there will usually be an opportunity to check the assumptions which 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1059: trailing whitespace.
+underlay the original design and sample size calc ulations. This may be particularly important 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1060: trailing whitespace.
+if the trial specifications have been made on  preliminary and/or uncertain information. An 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1061: trailing whitespace.
+interim check conducted on the blinded data may re veal that overall response variances, event 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1062: trailing whitespace.
+rates or survival experience are not as anticipated. A revised sample size may then be 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1066: trailing whitespace.
+Â© EMEA 2006  22 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1067: trailing whitespace.
+ 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1068: trailing whitespace.
+calculated using suitably modified assumptions , and should be justified and documented in a 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1069: trailing whitespace.
+protocol amendment and in the clinical study report. The steps taken to preserve blindness 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1070: trailing whitespace.
+and the consequences, if any, for the type I e rror and the width of confidence intervals should 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1071: trailing whitespace.
+be explained. The potential need for re-estima tion of the sample size should be envisaged in 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1072: trailing whitespace.
+the protocol whenever possible (see Section 3.5). 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1073: trailing whitespace.
+4.5 Interim Analysis and Early Stopping 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1074: trailing whitespace.
+An interim analysis is any analysis intended to compare treatment arms with respect to 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1075: trailing whitespace.
+efficacy or safety at any time prior to formal completion of a trial. Because the number, 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1076: trailing whitespace.
+methods and consequences of these comparisons affect the interpretation of the trial, all 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1077: trailing whitespace.
+interim analyses should be carefully planned in advance and described in the protocol. Special 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1078: trailing whitespace.
+circumstances may dictate the need for an interim analysis that was not defined at the start of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1079: trailing whitespace.
+a trial. In these cases, a protocol amendment describing the interim analysis should be 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1080: trailing whitespace.
+completed prior to unblinded access to treatment comparison data. When an interim analysis 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1081: trailing whitespace.
+is planned with the intention of deciding whether or not to terminate a trial, this is usually 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1082: trailing whitespace.
+accomplished by the use of a group sequential design which employs statistical monitoring 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1083: trailing whitespace.
+schemes as guidelines (see Section 3.4). The goal of such an interim analysis is to stop the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1084: trailing whitespace.
+trial early if the superiority of the treatmen t under study is clearly established, if the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1085: trailing whitespace.
+demonstration of a relevant treatment difference has become unlikely or if unacceptable 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1086: trailing whitespace.
+adverse effects are apparent. Generally, boundaries for monitoring efficacy require more 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1087: trailing whitespace.
+evidence to terminate a trial early (i.e. they  are more conservative) than boundaries for 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1088: trailing whitespace.
+monitoring safety. When the trial design and monitoring objective involve multiple endpoints 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1089: trailing whitespace.
+then this aspect of multiplicity may also need to be taken into account. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1090: trailing whitespace.
+The protocol should describe the schedule of inte rim analyses, or at least the considerations 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1091: trailing whitespace.
+which will govern its generation, for example if flexible alpha spending function approaches 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1092: trailing whitespace.
+are to be employed; further details may be given in a protocol amendment before the time of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1093: trailing whitespace.
+the first interim analysis. The stopping guide lines and their properties should be clearly 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1094: trailing whitespace.
+described in the protocol or amendments. The potential effects of early stopping on the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1095: trailing whitespace.
+analysis of other important variables should also be considered. This material should be 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1096: trailing whitespace.
+written or approved by the Data Monitoring Co mmittee (see Section 4.6), when the trial has 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1097: trailing whitespace.
+one. Deviations from the planned procedure always  bear the potential of invalidating the trial 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1098: trailing whitespace.
+results. If it becomes necessary to make changes to the trial, any consequent changes to the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1099: trailing whitespace.
+statistical procedures should be specified in an amendment to the protocol at the earliest 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1100: trailing whitespace.
+opportunity, especially discussing the impact on any analysis and inferences that such 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1101: trailing whitespace.
+changes may cause. The procedures selected should always ensure that the overall probability 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1102: trailing whitespace.
+of type I error is controlled. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1103: trailing whitespace.
+The execution of an interim analysis should be  a completely confidential process because 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1104: trailing whitespace.
+unblinded data and results are potentially involved. All staff involved in the conduct of the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1105: trailing whitespace.
+trial should remain blind to the results of such analyses, because of the possibility that their 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1106: trailing whitespace.
+attitudes to the trial will be modified and cause changes in the characteristics of patients to be 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1107: trailing whitespace.
+recruited or biases in treatment comparisons. Th is principle may be applied to all investigator 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1108: trailing whitespace.
+staff and to staff employed by the sponsor except for those who are directly involved in the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1109: trailing whitespace.
+execution of the interim analysis. Investigators should only be informed about the decision to 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1110: trailing whitespace.
+continue or to discontinue the trial, or to implement modifications to trial procedures. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1111: trailing whitespace.
+Most clinical trials intended to support the effi cacy and safety of an investigational product 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1112: trailing whitespace.
+should proceed to full completion of planned sample size accrual; trials should be stopped 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1113: trailing whitespace.
+early only for ethical reasons or if the power  is no longer acceptable. However, it is 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1114: trailing whitespace.
+recognised that drug development plans involve the need for sponsor access to comparative 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1115: trailing whitespace.
+treatment data for a variety of reasons, such as planning other trials. It is also recognised that 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1116: trailing whitespace.
+only a subset of trials will involve the study of  serious life-threatening outcomes or mortality 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1120: trailing whitespace.
+Â© EMEA 2006  23 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1121: trailing whitespace.
+ 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1122: trailing whitespace.
+which may need sequential monitoring of accruing comparative treatment effects for ethical 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1123: trailing whitespace.
+reasons. In either of these situations, plans for interim statistical analysis should be in place in 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1124: trailing whitespace.
+the protocol or in protocol amendments prior to the unblinded access to comparative 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1125: trailing whitespace.
+treatment data in order to deal with the potential statistical and operational bias that may be 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1126: trailing whitespace.
+introduced. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1127: trailing whitespace.
+For many clinical trials of investigational pr oducts, especially those that have major public 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1128: trailing whitespace.
+health significance, the responsibility for monito ring comparisons of efficacy and/or safety 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1129: trailing whitespace.
+outcomes should be assigned to an external independent group, often called an Independent 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1130: trailing whitespace.
+Data Monitoring Committee (IDMC), a Data and Safety Monitoring Board or a Data 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1131: trailing whitespace.
+Monitoring Committee whose responsibilities should be clearly described. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1132: trailing whitespace.
+When a sponsor assumes the role of monitoring efficacy or safety comparisons and therefore 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1133: trailing whitespace.
+has access to unblinded comparative information, particular care should be taken to protect 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1134: trailing whitespace.
+the integrity of the trial and to manage and limit appropriately the sharing of information. The 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1135: trailing whitespace.
+sponsor should assure and document that the internal monitoring committee has complied 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1136: trailing whitespace.
+with written standard operating procedures an d that minutes of decision making meetings 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1137: trailing whitespace.
+including records of interim results are maintained. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1138: trailing whitespace.
+Any interim analysis that is not planned approp riately (with or without the consequences of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1139: trailing whitespace.
+stopping the trial early) may flaw the results of a trial and possibly weaken confidence in the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1140: trailing whitespace.
+conclusions drawn. Therefore, such analyses should be avoided. If unplanned interim analysis 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1141: trailing whitespace.
+is conducted, the clinical study report should explain why it was necessary, the degree to 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1142: trailing whitespace.
+which blindness had to be broken, provide an assessment of the potential magnitude of bias 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1143: trailing whitespace.
+introduced, and the impact on the interpretation of the results.  
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1144: trailing whitespace.
+4.6 Role of Independent Data Monitoring Committee (IDMC) 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1145: trailing whitespace.
+(see Sections 1.25 and 5.52 of ICH E6) 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1146: trailing whitespace.
+An IDMC may be established by the sponsor to assess at intervals the progress of a clinical 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1147: trailing whitespace.
+trial, safety data, and critical efficacy variables and recommend to the sponsor whether to 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1148: trailing whitespace.
+continue, modify or terminate a trial. The ID MC should have written operating procedures 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1149: trailing whitespace.
+and maintain records of all its meetings, incl uding interim results; th ese should be available 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1150: trailing whitespace.
+for review when the trial is complete. The inde pendence of the IDMC is intended to control 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1151: trailing whitespace.
+the sharing of important comparative information and to protect the integrity of the clinical 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1152: trailing whitespace.
+trial from adverse impact resulting from access to  trial information. The IDMC is a separate 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1153: trailing whitespace.
+entity from an Institutional Review Board (IRB) or an Independent Ethics Committee (IEC), 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1154: trailing whitespace.
+and its composition should include clinical tria l scientists knowledgeable in the appropriate 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1155: trailing whitespace.
+disciplines including statistics. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1156: trailing whitespace.
+When there are sponsor representatives on the IDMC, their role should be clearly defined in 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1157: trailing whitespace.
+the operating procedures of the committee (for example, covering whether or not they can 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1158: trailing whitespace.
+vote on key issues). Since these sponsor staff would have access to unblinded information, the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1159: trailing whitespace.
+procedures should also address the control of di ssemination of interim trial results within the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1160: trailing whitespace.
+sponsor organisation. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1161: trailing whitespace.
+V DATA ANALYSIS CONSIDERATIONS 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1162: trailing whitespace.
+5.1 Prespecification of the Analysis  
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1163: trailing whitespace.
+When designing a clinical trial the principal featur es of the eventual statistical analysis of the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1164: trailing whitespace.
+data should be described in the statistical sect ion of the protocol. This section should include 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1165: trailing whitespace.
+all the principal features of the proposed confir matory analysis of the primary variable(s) and 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1169: trailing whitespace.
+Â© EMEA 2006  24 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1170: trailing whitespace.
+ 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1171: trailing whitespace.
+the way in which anticipated analysis problems will be handled. In case of exploratory trials 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1172: trailing whitespace.
+this section could describe more general principles and directions. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1173: trailing whitespace.
+The statistical analysis plan (see Glossary) may be written as a separate document to be 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1174: trailing whitespace.
+completed after finalising the protocol. In this document, a more technical and detailed 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1175: trailing whitespace.
+elaboration of the principal features stated in the protocol may be included (see section 7.1). 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1176: trailing whitespace.
+The plan may include detailed procedures for ex ecuting the statistical analysis of the primary 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1177: trailing whitespace.
+and secondary variables and other data. The plan  should be reviewed and possibly updated as 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1178: trailing whitespace.
+a result of the blind review of the data (see 7.1 for definition) and should be finalised before 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1179: trailing whitespace.
+breaking the blind. Formal records should be kept  of when the statistical analysis plan was 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1180: trailing whitespace.
+finalised as well as when the blind was subsequently broken. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1181: trailing whitespace.
+If the blind review suggests changes to the prin cipal features stated in the protocol, these 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1182: trailing whitespace.
+should be documented in a protocol amendment. Otherwise, it will suffice to update the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1183: trailing whitespace.
+statistical analysis plan with the considerati ons suggested from the blind review. Only results 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1184: trailing whitespace.
+from analyses envisaged in the protocol (i ncluding amendments) can be regarded as 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1185: trailing whitespace.
+confirmatory. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1186: trailing whitespace.
+In the statistical section of the clinical study report the statistical methodology should be 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1187: trailing whitespace.
+clearly described including when in the clinic al trial process methodology decisions were 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1188: trailing whitespace.
+made (see ICH E3). 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1189: trailing whitespace.
+5.2 Analysis Sets 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1190: trailing whitespace.
+The set of subjects whose data are to be included in the main analyses should be defined in 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1191: trailing whitespace.
+the statistical section of the protocol. In a ddition, documentation for all subjects for whom 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1192: trailing whitespace.
+trial procedures (e.g. run-in period) were initia ted may be useful. The content of this subject 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1193: trailing whitespace.
+documentation depends on detailed features of the particular trial, but at least demographic 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1194: trailing whitespace.
+and baseline data on disease status should be collected whenever possible.  
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1195: trailing whitespace.
+If all subjects randomised into a clinical trial satisfied all entry criteria, followed all trial 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1196: trailing whitespace.
+procedures perfectly with no lo sses to follow-up, and provided complete data records, then 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1197: trailing whitespace.
+the set of subjects to be included in the analysis would be self-evident. The design and 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1198: trailing whitespace.
+conduct of a trial should aim to approach this ideal  as closely as possible, but, in practice, it is 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1199: trailing whitespace.
+doubtful if it can ever be fully achieved. Hence,  the statistical section of the protocol should 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1200: trailing whitespace.
+address anticipated problems prospectively in te rms of how these affect the subjects and data 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1201: trailing whitespace.
+to be analysed. The protocol should also specify procedures aimed at minimising any 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1202: trailing whitespace.
+anticipated irregularities in study conduct that might impair a satisfactory analysis, including 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1203: trailing whitespace.
+various types of protocol violations, withdr awals and missing values. The protocol should 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1204: trailing whitespace.
+consider ways both to reduce the frequency of such problems, and also to handle the problems 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1205: trailing whitespace.
+that do occur in the analysis of data. Possible amendments to the way in which the analysis 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1206: trailing whitespace.
+will deal with protocol violations should be id entified during the blind review. It is desirable 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1207: trailing whitespace.
+to identify any important protocol violation with respect to the time when it occurred, its 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1208: trailing whitespace.
+cause and influence on the trial result. The frequen cy and type of protocol violations, missing 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1209: trailing whitespace.
+values, and other problems should be documented in the clinical study report and their 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1210: trailing whitespace.
+potential influence on the trial results should be described (see ICH E3). 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1211: trailing whitespace.
+Decisions concerning the analysis set should be guided by the following principles : 1) to 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1212: trailing whitespace.
+minimise bias, and 2) to avoid inflation of type I error.  
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1213: trailing whitespace.
+5.2.1 Full Analysis Set  
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1214: trailing whitespace.
+The intention-to-treat (see Glossary) principle implies that the primary analysis should 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1215: trailing whitespace.
+include all randomised subjects. Compliance with  this principle would necessitate complete 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1216: trailing whitespace.
+follow-up of all randomised subjects for study outcomes. In practice this ideal may be 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1217: trailing whitespace.
+difficult to achieve, for reasons to be described. In this document the term 'full analysis set' is 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1221: trailing whitespace.
+Â© EMEA 2006  25 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1222: trailing whitespace.
+ 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1223: trailing whitespace.
+used to describe the analysis set which is as co mplete as possible and as close as possible to 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1224: trailing whitespace.
+the intention-to-treat ideal of including all ra ndomised subjects. Preservation of the initial 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1225: trailing whitespace.
+randomisation in analysis is important in preventing bias and in providing a secure foundation 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1226: trailing whitespace.
+for statistical tests. In many clinical trials the use of the full analysis set provides a 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1227: trailing whitespace.
+conservative strategy. Under many circumstances  it may also provide estimates of treatment 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1228: trailing whitespace.
+effects which are more likely to mirror those observed in subsequent practice. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1229: trailing whitespace.
+There are a limited number of circumstances that might lead to excluding randomised subjects 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1230: trailing whitespace.
+from the full analysis set including the failure to satisfy major entry criteria (eligibility 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1231: trailing whitespace.
+violations), the failure to take at least one dos e of trial medication and the lack of any data 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1232: trailing whitespace.
+post randomisation. Such exclusions should always  be justified. Subject s who fail to satisfy 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1233: trailing whitespace.
+an entry criterion may be excluded from the analysis without the possibility of introducing 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1234: trailing whitespace.
+bias only under the following circumstances: 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1235: trailing whitespace.
+i) the entry criterion was measured prior to randomisation; 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1236: trailing whitespace.
+ii) the detection of the relevant eligibility violations can be made completely objectively; 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1237: trailing whitespace.
+iii) all subjects receive equal scrutiny for eligibility violations; (This may be difficult to 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1238: trailing whitespace.
+ensure in an open-label study, or even in a double-blind study if the data are unblinded 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1239: trailing whitespace.
+prior to this scrutiny, emphasising the importance of the blind review.) 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1240: trailing whitespace.
+iv) all detected violations of the pa rticular entry criterion are excluded. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1241: trailing whitespace.
+In some situations, it may be reasonable to el iminate from the set of all randomised subjects 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1242: trailing whitespace.
+any subject who took no trial medication. The in tention-to-treat principle would be preserved 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1243: trailing whitespace.
+despite the exclusion of these patients provided, for example, that the decision of whether or 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1244: trailing whitespace.
+not to begin treatment could not be influenced by knowledge of the assigned treatment. In 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1245: trailing whitespace.
+other situations it may be necessary to elimin ate from the set of all randomised subjects any 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1246: trailing whitespace.
+subject without data post randomisation. No anal ysis is complete unless the potential biases 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1247: trailing whitespace.
+arising from these specific exclusions, or any others, are addressed. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1248: trailing whitespace.
+When the full analysis set of subjects is used , violations of the protocol that occur after 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1249: trailing whitespace.
+randomisation may have an impact on the data  and conclusions, particularly if their 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1250: trailing whitespace.
+occurrence is related to treatment assignment. In most respects it is appropriate to include the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1251: trailing whitespace.
+data from such subjects in the analysis, consistent with the intention-to-treat principle. Special 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1252: trailing whitespace.
+problems arise in connection with subjects withdrawn from treatment after receiving one or 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1253: trailing whitespace.
+more doses who provide no data after this point, and subjects otherwise lost to follow-up, 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1254: trailing whitespace.
+because failure to include these subjects in th e full analysis set may seriously undermine the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1255: trailing whitespace.
+approach. Measurements of primary variables made at the time of the loss to follow-up of a 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1256: trailing whitespace.
+subject for any reason, or subsequently collected in accordance with the intended schedule of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1257: trailing whitespace.
+assessments in the protocol, are valuable in this  context; subsequent co llection is especially 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1258: trailing whitespace.
+important in studies where the primary variable is mortality or serious morbidity. The 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1259: trailing whitespace.
+intention to collect data in this way should be described in the protocol. Imputation 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1260: trailing whitespace.
+techniques, ranging from the carrying forward of the last observation to the use of complex 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1261: trailing whitespace.
+mathematical models, may also be used in an attempt to compensate for missing data. Other 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1262: trailing whitespace.
+methods employed to ensure the availability of measurements of primary variables for every 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1263: trailing whitespace.
+subject in the full analysis set may require some assumptions about the subjects' outcomes or 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1264: trailing whitespace.
+a simpler choice of outcome (e.g. success / fail ure). The use of any of these strategies should 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1265: trailing whitespace.
+be described and justified in the statistical section of the protocol and the assumptions 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1266: trailing whitespace.
+underlying any mathematical models employed should be clearly explained. It is also 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1267: trailing whitespace.
+important to demonstrate the robustness of th e corresponding results of analysis especially 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1268: trailing whitespace.
+when the strategy in question could itself lead to biased estimates of treatment effects.  
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1269: trailing whitespace.
+Because of the unpredictability of some problems, it may sometimes be preferable to defer 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1270: trailing whitespace.
+detailed consideration of the manner of dealing with irregularities until the blind review of the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1271: trailing whitespace.
+data at the end of the trial, and, if so, this should be stated in the protocol. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1275: trailing whitespace.
+Â© EMEA 2006  26 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1276: trailing whitespace.
+ 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1277: trailing whitespace.
+5.2.2 Per Protocol Set 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1278: trailing whitespace.
+The 'per protocol' set of subjects, sometimes described as the 'valid cases', the 'efficacy' 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1279: trailing whitespace.
+sample or the 'evaluable subjects' sample, defines a subset of the subjects in the full analysis 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1280: trailing whitespace.
+set who are more compliant with the protocol and is characterised by criteria such as the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1281: trailing whitespace.
+following: 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1282: trailing whitespace.
+i) the completion of a certain pre-specified minimal exposure to the treatment regimen; 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1283: trailing whitespace.
+ii) the availability of measurements of the primary variable(s); 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1284: trailing whitespace.
+iii) the absence of any major protocol violati ons including the violation of entry criteria.  
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1285: trailing whitespace.
+The precise reasons for excluding subjects from the per protocol set should be fully defined 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1286: trailing whitespace.
+and documented before breaking the blind in a manner appropriate to the circumstances of the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1287: trailing whitespace.
+specific trial. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1288: trailing whitespace.
+The use of the per protocol set may maximise the opportunity for a new treatment to show 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1289: trailing whitespace.
+additional efficacy in the analysis, and most closely reflects the scientific model underlying 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1290: trailing whitespace.
+the protocol. However, the corresponding test of  the hypothesis and estimate of the treatment 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1291: trailing whitespace.
+effect may or may not be conservative depending on the trial; the bias, which may be severe, 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1292: trailing whitespace.
+arises from the fact that adherence to the study protocol may be related to treatment and 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1293: trailing whitespace.
+outcome.  
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1294: trailing whitespace.
+The problems that lead to the exclusion of subjects to create the per protocol set, and other 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1295: trailing whitespace.
+protocol violations, should be fully identified and summarised. Relevant protocol violations 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1296: trailing whitespace.
+may include errors in treatment assignment, the use of excluded medication, poor compliance, 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1297: trailing whitespace.
+loss to follow-up and missing data. It is good pr actice to assess the pattern of such problems 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1298: trailing whitespace.
+among the treatment groups with respect to frequency and time to occurrence. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1299: trailing whitespace.
+5.2.3 Roles of the Different Analysis Sets 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1300: trailing whitespace.
+In general, it is advantageous to demonstrate a lack of sensitivity of the principal trial results 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1301: trailing whitespace.
+to alternative choices of the set of subjects an alysed. In confirmatory trials it is usually 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1302: trailing whitespace.
+appropriate to plan to conduct both an analysis  of the full analysis set and a per protocol 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1303: trailing whitespace.
+analysis, so that any differences between them can be the subject of explicit discussion and 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1304: trailing whitespace.
+interpretation. In some cases, it may be desirabl e to plan further exploration of the sensitivity 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1305: trailing whitespace.
+of conclusions to the choice of the set of subj ects analysed. When the full analysis set and the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1306: trailing whitespace.
+per protocol set lead to essentially the same conclusions, confidence in the trial results is 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1307: trailing whitespace.
+increased, bearing in mind, however, that the need to exclude a substantial proportion of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1308: trailing whitespace.
+subjects from the per protocol analysis throws some doubt on the overall validity of the trial. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1309: trailing whitespace.
+The full analysis set and the per protocol set play different roles in superiority trials (which 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1310: trailing whitespace.
+seek to show the investigational product to be superior), and in equivalence or non-inferiority 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1311: trailing whitespace.
+trials (which seek to show the investigational product to be comparable, see section 3.3.2). In 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1312: trailing whitespace.
+superiority trials the full analysis set is used in the primary analysis (apart from exceptional 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1313: trailing whitespace.
+circumstances) because it tends to avoid over-optimistic estimates of efficacy resulting from a 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1314: trailing whitespace.
+per protocol analysis, since the non-compliers included in the full analysis set will generally 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1315: trailing whitespace.
+diminish the estimated treatment effect. However, in an equivalence or non-inferiority trial 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1316: trailing whitespace.
+use of the full analysis set is generally not cons ervative and its role should be considered very 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1317: trailing whitespace.
+carefully.  
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1318: trailing whitespace.
+5.3 Missing Values and Outliers 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1319: trailing whitespace.
+Missing values represent a potential source of bias  in a clinical trial. Hence, every effort 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1320: trailing whitespace.
+should be undertaken to fulfil all the requirements of the protocol concerning the collection 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1321: trailing whitespace.
+and management of data. In reality, however, there will almost always be some missing data. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1322: trailing whitespace.
+A trial may be regarded as valid, nonetheless,  provided the methods of  dealing with missing 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1326: trailing whitespace.
+Â© EMEA 2006  27 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1327: trailing whitespace.
+ 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1328: trailing whitespace.
+values are sensible, and particularly if t hose methods are pre-defined in the protocol. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1329: trailing whitespace.
+Definition of methods may be refined by updating this aspect in the statistical analysis plan 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1330: trailing whitespace.
+during the blind review. Unfortunately, no uni versally applicable methods of handling 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1331: trailing whitespace.
+missing values can be recommended. An investigation should be made concerning the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1332: trailing whitespace.
+sensitivity of the results of analysis to the method of handling missing values, especially if the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1333: trailing whitespace.
+number of missing values is substantial. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1334: trailing whitespace.
+A similar approach should be adopted to explor ing the influence of ou tliers, the statistical 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1335: trailing whitespace.
+definition of which is, to some extent, arbitrary. Clear identification of a particular value as an 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1336: trailing whitespace.
+outlier is most convincing when justified medically as well as statistically, and the medical 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1337: trailing whitespace.
+context will then often define the appropriate  action. Any outlier procedure set out in the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1338: trailing whitespace.
+protocol or the statistical anal ysis plan should be such as not to favour any treatment group a 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1339: trailing whitespace.
+priori. Once again, this aspect of the analys is can be usefully updated during blind review. If 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1340: trailing whitespace.
+no procedure for dealing with outliers was foreseen  in the trial protocol, one analysis with the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1341: trailing whitespace.
+actual values and at least one other analysis eliminating or reducing the outlier effect should 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1342: trailing whitespace.
+be performed and differences between their results discussed. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1343: trailing whitespace.
+5.4 Data Transformation 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1344: trailing whitespace.
+The decision to transform key variables prior to analysis is best made during the design of the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1345: trailing whitespace.
+trial on the basis of similar data from earlier clin ical trials. Transformations (e.g. square root, 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1346: trailing whitespace.
+logarithm) should be specified in the protocol  and a rationale provided, especially for the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1347: trailing whitespace.
+primary variable(s). The general principles guid ing the use of transformations to ensure that 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1348: trailing whitespace.
+the assumptions underlying the statistical methods are met are to be found in standard texts; 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1349: trailing whitespace.
+conventions for particular variables have been developed in a number of specific clinical 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1350: trailing whitespace.
+areas. The decision on whether and how to transform a variable should be influenced by the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1351: trailing whitespace.
+preference for a scale which facilitates clinical interpretation. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1352: trailing whitespace.
+Similar considerations apply to other derived variables, such as the use of change from 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1353: trailing whitespace.
+baseline, percentage change from baseline, the 'a rea under the curve' of repeated measures, or 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1354: trailing whitespace.
+the ratio of two different variables. Subseque nt clinical interpretation should be carefully 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1355: trailing whitespace.
+considered, and the derivation should be justified in the protocol. Closely related points are 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1356: trailing whitespace.
+made in Section 2.2.2. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1357: trailing whitespace.
+5.5 Estimation, Confidence Intervals and Hypothesis Testing 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1358: trailing whitespace.
+The statistical section of the protocol should specify the hypotheses that are to be tested 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1359: trailing whitespace.
+and/or the treatment effects which are to be estimated in order to satisfy the primary 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1360: trailing whitespace.
+objectives of the trial. The statistical methods to be used to accomplish these tasks should be 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1361: trailing whitespace.
+described for the primary (and preferably the secondary) variables, and the underlying 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1362: trailing whitespace.
+statistical model should be made clear. Estima tes of treatment effects should be accompanied 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1363: trailing whitespace.
+by confidence intervals, whenever possible, and the way in which these will be calculated 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1364: trailing whitespace.
+should be identified. A description should be given of any intentions to use baseline data to 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1365: trailing whitespace.
+improve precision or to adjust estimates for potential baseline differences, for example by 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1366: trailing whitespace.
+means of analysis of covariance.  
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1367: trailing whitespace.
+It is important to clarify whether one- or two- sided tests of statistical  significance will be 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1368: trailing whitespace.
+used, and in particular to justify prospectivel y the use of one-sided te sts. If hypothesis tests 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1369: trailing whitespace.
+are not considered appropriate, then the alternative process for arriving at statistical 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1370: trailing whitespace.
+conclusions should be given. The issue of one-si ded or two-sided approaches to inference is 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1371: trailing whitespace.
+controversial and a diversity of views can be found in the statistical literature. The approach 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1373: trailing whitespace.
+sided tests is preferable in regulatory settings . This promotes consiste ncy with the two-sided 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1377: trailing whitespace.
+Â© EMEA 2006  28 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1378: trailing whitespace.
+ 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1379: trailing whitespace.
+confidence intervals that are generally appropr iate for estimating the possible size of the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1380: trailing whitespace.
+difference between two treatments. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1381: trailing whitespace.
+The particular statistical model chosen shoul d reflect the current state of medical and 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1382: trailing whitespace.
+statistical knowledge about the variables to be analysed as well as the statistical design of the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1383: trailing whitespace.
+trial. All effects to be fitted in the analysis (for example in analysis of variance models) 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1384: trailing whitespace.
+should be fully specified, and the manner, if any, in which this set of effects might be 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1385: trailing whitespace.
+modified in response to preliminary results should be explained. The same considerations 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1386: trailing whitespace.
+apply to the set of covariates fitted in an analysis of covariance. (See also Section 5.7.). In the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1387: trailing whitespace.
+choice of statistical methods due attention should be paid to the statistical distribution of both 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1388: trailing whitespace.
+primary and secondary variables. When making this choice (for example between parametric 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1389: trailing whitespace.
+and non-parametric methods) it is important to bear in mind the need to provide statistical 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1390: trailing whitespace.
+estimates of the size of treatment effects together with confidence intervals (in addition to 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1391: trailing whitespace.
+significance tests). 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1392: trailing whitespace.
+The primary analysis of the primary variable should be clearly distinguished from supporting 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1393: trailing whitespace.
+analyses of the primary or secondary variables. Within the statistical section of the protocol or 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1394: trailing whitespace.
+the statistical analysis plan there should also be  an outline of the way in which data other than 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1395: trailing whitespace.
+the primary and secondary variables will be summarised and reported. This should include a 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1396: trailing whitespace.
+reference to any approaches adopted for the purpose of achieving consistency of analysis 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1397: trailing whitespace.
+across a range of trials, for example for safety data.  
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1398: trailing whitespace.
+Modelling approaches that incorporate information on known pharmacological parameters, 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1399: trailing whitespace.
+the extent of protocol compliance for individual  subjects or other biologically based data may 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1400: trailing whitespace.
+provide valuable insights into actual or potential efficacy, especially with regard to estimation 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1401: trailing whitespace.
+of treatment effects. The assumptions underlying such models should always be clearly 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1402: trailing whitespace.
+identified, and the limitations of any conclusions should be carefully described. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1403: trailing whitespace.
+5.6 Adjustment of Signific ance and Confidence Levels 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1404: trailing whitespace.
+When multiplicity is present, the usual frequentist approach to the analysis of clinical trial 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1405: trailing whitespace.
+data may necessitate an adjustment to the type I error. Multiplicity may arise, for example, 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1406: trailing whitespace.
+from multiple primary variables (see Section 2.2.2), multiple comparisons of treatments, 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1407: trailing whitespace.
+repeated evaluation over time and/or interim analyses (see Section 4.5). Methods to avoid or 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1408: trailing whitespace.
+reduce multiplicity are sometimes preferable when available, such as the identification of the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1409: trailing whitespace.
+key primary variable (multiple variables), the choi ce of a critical treatment contrast (multiple 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1410: trailing whitespace.
+comparisons), the use of a summary measure such as â€˜area under the curveâ€™ (repeated 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1411: trailing whitespace.
+measures). In confirmatory analyses, any aspect s of multiplicity which remain after steps of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1412: trailing whitespace.
+this kind have been taken should be identified in the protocol; adjustment should always be 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1413: trailing whitespace.
+considered and the details of any adjustment procedure or an explanation of why adjustment 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1414: trailing whitespace.
+is not thought to be necessary should be set out in the analysis plan. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1415: trailing whitespace.
+5.7 Subgroups, Interactions and Covariates 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1416: trailing whitespace.
+The primary variable(s) is often systematical ly related to other influences apart from 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1417: trailing whitespace.
+treatment. For example, there may be relationships  to covariates such as age and sex, or there 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1418: trailing whitespace.
+may be differences between specific subgroups  of subjects such as those treated at the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1419: trailing whitespace.
+different centres of a multicentre trial. In some instances an adjustment for the influence of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1420: trailing whitespace.
+covariates or for subgroup effects is an integral  part of the planned analysis and hence should 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1421: trailing whitespace.
+be set out in the protocol. Pre-trial deliberati ons should identify those covariates and factors 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1422: trailing whitespace.
+expected to have an important influence on the primary variable(s), and should consider how 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1423: trailing whitespace.
+to account for these in the analysis in order to improve precision and to compensate for any 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1424: trailing whitespace.
+lack of balance between treatment groups. If one or more factors are used to stratify the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1425: trailing whitespace.
+design, it is appropriate to account for those fa ctors in the analysis. When the potential value 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1429: trailing whitespace.
+Â© EMEA 2006  29 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1430: trailing whitespace.
+ 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1431: trailing whitespace.
+of an adjustment is in doubt, it is often advisable to nominate the unadjusted analysis as the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1432: trailing whitespace.
+one for primary attention, the adjusted analysis  being supportive. Special attention should be 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1433: trailing whitespace.
+paid to centre effects and to the role of baseline measurements of the primary variable. It is 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1434: trailing whitespace.
+not advisable to adjust the main analyses for covariates measured after randomisation because 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1435: trailing whitespace.
+they may be affected by the treatments. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1436: trailing whitespace.
+The treatment effect itself may also vary with subgroup or covariate - for example, the effect 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1437: trailing whitespace.
+may decrease with age or may be larger in a particular diagnostic category of subjects. In 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1438: trailing whitespace.
+some cases such interactions are anticipated or are of particular prior interest (e.g. geriatrics), 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1439: trailing whitespace.
+and hence a subgroup analysis, or a statistical mo del including interactions, is part of the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1440: trailing whitespace.
+planned confirmatory analysis. In most cases, however, subgroup or interaction analyses are 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1441: trailing whitespace.
+exploratory and should be clearly identified as such; they should explore the uniformity of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1442: trailing whitespace.
+any treatment effects found overall. In general,  such analyses should proceed first through the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1443: trailing whitespace.
+addition of interaction terms to the statistical model in question, complemented by additional 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1444: trailing whitespace.
+exploratory analysis within relevant subgroups of  subjects, or within strata defined by the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1445: trailing whitespace.
+covariates. When exploratory, these analyses should be interpreted cautiously; any conclusion 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1446: trailing whitespace.
+of treatment efficacy (or lack thereof) or safety based solely on exploratory subgroup analyses 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1447: trailing whitespace.
+are unlikely to be accepted. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1448: trailing whitespace.
+5.8 Integrity of Data and Computer Software Validity 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1449: trailing whitespace.
+The credibility of the numerical results of the analysis depends on the quality and validity of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1450: trailing whitespace.
+the methods and software (both internally and externally written) used both for data 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1451: trailing whitespace.
+management (data entry, storage, verification, correction and retrieval) and also for 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1452: trailing whitespace.
+processing the data statistically. Data manage ment activities should therefore be based on 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1453: trailing whitespace.
+thorough and effective standard operating proced ures. The computer software used for data 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1454: trailing whitespace.
+management and statistical analysis should be reliable, and documentation of appropriate 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1455: trailing whitespace.
+software testing procedures should be available. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1456: trailing whitespace.
+VI EVALUATION OF SAFETY AND TOLERABILITY  
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1457: trailing whitespace.
+6.1 Scope of Evaluation 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1458: trailing whitespace.
+In all clinical trials evaluation of safety and tolerability (see Glossary) constitutes an 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1459: trailing whitespace.
+important element. In early phases this evaluation is mostly of an exploratory nature, and is 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1460: trailing whitespace.
+only sensitive to frank expressions of toxicity, whereas in later phases the establishment of the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1461: trailing whitespace.
+safety and tolerability profile of a drug can be  characterised more fully in larger samples of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1462: trailing whitespace.
+subjects. Later phase controlled trials represent an important means of exploring in an 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1463: trailing whitespace.
+unbiased manner any new potential adverse effects, even if such trials generally lack power in 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1464: trailing whitespace.
+this respect. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1465: trailing whitespace.
+Certain trials may be designed with the purpose of making specific claims about superiority or 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1466: trailing whitespace.
+equivalence with regard to safety and tolerability compared to another drug or to another dose 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1467: trailing whitespace.
+of the investigational drug. Such specific claims should be supported by relevant evidence 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1468: trailing whitespace.
+from confirmatory trials, similar to that necessary for corresponding efficacy claims. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1469: trailing whitespace.
+6.2 Choice of Variables and Data Collection 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1470: trailing whitespace.
+In any clinical trial the methods and measurements chosen to evaluate the safety and 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1471: trailing whitespace.
+tolerability of a drug will depend on a number of factors, including knowledge of the adverse 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1472: trailing whitespace.
+effects of closely related drugs, information from non-clinical and earlier clinical trials and 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1473: trailing whitespace.
+possible consequences of the pharmacodynamic/ pharmacokinetic properties of the particular 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1474: trailing whitespace.
+drug, the mode of administration, the type of subjects to be studied, and the duration of the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1478: trailing whitespace.
+Â© EMEA 2006  30 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1479: trailing whitespace.
+ 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1480: trailing whitespace.
+trial. Laboratory tests concerning clinical chem istry and haematology, vital signs, and clinical 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1481: trailing whitespace.
+adverse events (diseases, signs and symptoms) usually form the main body of the safety and 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1482: trailing whitespace.
+tolerability data. The occurrence of serious a dverse events and treatment discontinuations due 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1483: trailing whitespace.
+to adverse events are particularly important to register (see ICH E2A and ICH E3). 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1484: trailing whitespace.
+Furthermore, it is recommended that a consistent methodology be used for the data collection 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1485: trailing whitespace.
+and evaluation throughout a clinical trial program in order to facilitate the combining of data 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1486: trailing whitespace.
+from different trials. The use of a common adverse event dictionary is particularly important. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1487: trailing whitespace.
+This dictionary has a structure which gives the possibility to summarise the adverse event data 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1488: trailing whitespace.
+on three different levels; system-organ class, pr eferred term or included term (see Glossary). 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1489: trailing whitespace.
+The preferred term is the level on which adverse events usually are summarised, and preferred 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1490: trailing whitespace.
+terms belonging to the same system-organ class could then be brought together in the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1491: trailing whitespace.
+descriptive presentation of data (see ICH M1). 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1492: trailing whitespace.
+6.3 Set of Subjects to be Evaluated and Presentation of Data 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1493: trailing whitespace.
+For the overall safety and tole rability assessment, the set of subjects to be summarised is 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1494: trailing whitespace.
+usually defined as those subjects who received at least one dose of th e investigational drug. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1495: trailing whitespace.
+Safety and tolerability variables should be co llected as comprehensively as possible from 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1496: trailing whitespace.
+these subjects, including type of adverse even t, severity, onset and duration (see ICH E2B). 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1497: trailing whitespace.
+Additional safety and tolerability evaluations ma y be needed in specific subpopulations, such 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1498: trailing whitespace.
+as females, the elderly (see ICH E7), the severely ill, or those who have a common 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1499: trailing whitespace.
+concomitant treatment. These evaluations may need to address more specific issues (see ICH 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1500: trailing whitespace.
+E3).  
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1501: trailing whitespace.
+All safety and tolerability variables will need  attention during evaluation, and the broad 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1502: trailing whitespace.
+approach should be indicated in the protocol. All adverse events should be reported, whether 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1503: trailing whitespace.
+or not they are considered to be related to treatment. All available data in the study population 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1504: trailing whitespace.
+should be accounted for in the evaluation. De finitions of measurement units and reference 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1505: trailing whitespace.
+ranges of laboratory variables should be made  with care; if different units or different 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1506: trailing whitespace.
+reference ranges appear in the same trial (e.g. if  more than one laboratory is involved), then 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1507: trailing whitespace.
+measurements should be appropriately standard ised to allow a unified evaluation. Use of a 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1508: trailing whitespace.
+toxicity grading scale should be prespecified and justified. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1509: trailing whitespace.
+The incidence of a certain adverse event is us ually expressed in the form of a proportion 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1510: trailing whitespace.
+relating number of subjects experiencing events to number of subjects at risk. However, it is 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1511: trailing whitespace.
+not always self-evident how to assess incidence. For example, depending on the situation the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1512: trailing whitespace.
+number of exposed subjects or the extent of exposure (in person-years) could be considered 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1513: trailing whitespace.
+for the denominator. Whether the purpose of the calculation is to estimate a risk or to make a 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1514: trailing whitespace.
+comparison between treatment groups it is important that the definition is given in the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1515: trailing whitespace.
+protocol. This is especially important if l ong-term treatment is planned and a substantial 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1516: trailing whitespace.
+proportion of treatment withdrawals or deaths are expected. For such situations survival 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1517: trailing whitespace.
+analysis methods should be considered and cumu lative adverse event rates calculated in order 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1518: trailing whitespace.
+to avoid the risk of underestimation. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1519: trailing whitespace.
+In situations when there is a substantial background noise of signs and symptoms (e.g. in 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1520: trailing whitespace.
+psychiatric trials) one should consider ways of accounting for this in the estimation of risk for 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1521: trailing whitespace.
+different adverse events. One such method is to  make use of the 'treatment emergent' (see 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1522: trailing whitespace.
+Glossary) concept in which adverse events are recorded only if they emerge or worsen 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1523: trailing whitespace.
+relative to pretreatment baseline. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1524: trailing whitespace.
+Other methods to reduce the effect of the background noise may also be appropriate such as 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1525: trailing whitespace.
+ignoring adverse events of mild severity or requiring that an event should have been observed 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1526: trailing whitespace.
+at repeated visits to qualify for inclusion in the numerator. Such methods should be explained 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1527: trailing whitespace.
+and justified in the protocol. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1531: trailing whitespace.
+Â© EMEA 2006  31 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1532: trailing whitespace.
+ 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1533: trailing whitespace.
+6.4 Statistical Evaluation 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1534: trailing whitespace.
+The investigation of safety and tolerability is a multidimensional problem. Although some 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1535: trailing whitespace.
+specific adverse effects can usually be anticipated and specifically monitored for any drug, the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1536: trailing whitespace.
+range of possible adverse effects is very large, and new and unforeseeable effects are always 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1537: trailing whitespace.
+possible. Further, an adverse event experienced af ter a protocol violation, such as use of an 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1538: trailing whitespace.
+excluded medication, may introduce a bias. This background underlies the statistical 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1539: trailing whitespace.
+difficulties associated with the analytical evaluation of safety and tolerability of drugs, and 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1540: trailing whitespace.
+means that conclusive information from confirmatory clinical trials is the exception rather 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1541: trailing whitespace.
+than the rule. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1542: trailing whitespace.
+In most trials the safety and tolerability implications are best addressed by applying 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1543: trailing whitespace.
+descriptive statistical methods to the data, supplemented by calculation of confidence 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1544: trailing whitespace.
+intervals wherever this aids interpretation. It  is also valuable to make use of graphical 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1545: trailing whitespace.
+presentations in which patterns of adverse events are displayed both within treatment groups 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1546: trailing whitespace.
+and within subjects. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1547: trailing whitespace.
+The calculation of p-values is sometimes useful  either as an aid to evaluating a specific 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1548: trailing whitespace.
+difference of interest, or as a 'flagging' device applied to a large number of safety and 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1549: trailing whitespace.
+tolerability variables to highlight differences worth further attention. This is particularly 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1550: trailing whitespace.
+useful for laboratory data, which otherwise can be  difficult to summarise appropriately. It is 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1551: trailing whitespace.
+recommended that laboratory data be subjected to both a quantitative analysis, e.g. evaluation 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1552: trailing whitespace.
+of treatment means, and a qualitative analys is where counting of numbers above or below 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1553: trailing whitespace.
+certain thresholds are calculated.  
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1554: trailing whitespace.
+If hypothesis tests are used, statistical adjustments for multiplicity to quantify the type I error 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1555: trailing whitespace.
+are appropriate, but the type II error is usually of more concern. Care should be taken when 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1556: trailing whitespace.
+interpreting putative statistically significant findings when there is no multiplicity adjustment. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1557: trailing whitespace.
+In the majority of trials investigators are se eking to establish that there are no clinically 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1558: trailing whitespace.
+unacceptable differences in safety and tolerability compared with either a comparator drug or 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1559: trailing whitespace.
+a placebo. As is the case for non-inferiority or equivalence evaluation of efficacy the use of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1560: trailing whitespace.
+confidence intervals is preferred to hypothesis te sting in this situation. In this way, the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1561: trailing whitespace.
+considerable imprecision often arising from low frequencies of occurrence is clearly 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1562: trailing whitespace.
+demonstrated. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1563: trailing whitespace.
+6.5 Integrated Summary 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1564: trailing whitespace.
+The safety and tolerability properties of a drug are commonly summarised across trials 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1565: trailing whitespace.
+continuously during an investigational productâ€™s development and in particular at the time of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1566: trailing whitespace.
+a marketing application. The usefulness of th is summary, however, is dependent on adequate 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1567: trailing whitespace.
+and well-controlled individual trials with high data quality. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1568: trailing whitespace.
+The overall usefulness of a drug is always a question of balance between risk and benefit and 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1569: trailing whitespace.
+in a single trial such a perspective could also be considered, even if the assessment of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1570: trailing whitespace.
+risk/benefit usually is performed in the summary  of the entire clinical trial program. (See 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1571: trailing whitespace.
+section 7.2.2) 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1572: trailing whitespace.
+For more details on the reporting of safety and tolerability, see Chapter 12 of ICH E3.  
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1573: trailing whitespace.
+VII REPORTING 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1577: trailing whitespace.
+Â© EMEA 2006  32 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1578: trailing whitespace.
+ 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1579: trailing whitespace.
+7.1 Evaluation and Reporting 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1580: trailing whitespace.
+As stated in the Introduction, the structure and c ontent of clinical study reports is the subject 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1581: trailing whitespace.
+of ICH E3. That ICH guidance fully covers th e reporting of statistical work, appropriately 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1582: trailing whitespace.
+integrated with clinical and other material. The current section is therefore relatively brief. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1583: trailing whitespace.
+During the planning phase of a trial the principal features of the analysis should have been 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1584: trailing whitespace.
+specified in the protocol as described in Section 5. When the conduct of the trial is over and 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1585: trailing whitespace.
+the data are assembled and available for preliminary inspection, it is valuable to carry out the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1586: trailing whitespace.
+blind review of the planned analysis also desc ribed in Section 5. This pre-analysis review, 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1587: trailing whitespace.
+blinded to treatment, should cover decisions concerning, for example, the exclusion of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1588: trailing whitespace.
+subjects or data from the analysis sets; possi ble transformations may also be checked, and 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1589: trailing whitespace.
+outliers defined; important covariates identified in other recent research may be added to the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1590: trailing whitespace.
+model; the use of parametric or non-parame tric methods may be reconsidered. Decisions 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1591: trailing whitespace.
+made at this time should be described in th e report, and should be distinguished from those 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1592: trailing whitespace.
+made after the statistician has had access to the treatment codes, as blind decisions will 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1593: trailing whitespace.
+generally introduce less potential for bias. Stat isticians or other staff involved in unblinded 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1594: trailing whitespace.
+interim analysis should not participate in the blind review or in making modifications to the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1595: trailing whitespace.
+statistical analysis plan. When the blinding is compromised by the possibility that treatment 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1596: trailing whitespace.
+induced effects may be apparent in the data, special care will be needed for the blind review.  
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1597: trailing whitespace.
+Many of the more detailed aspects of presenta tion and tabulation should be finalised at or 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1598: trailing whitespace.
+about the time of the blind review so that by the time of the actual analysis full plans exist for 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1599: trailing whitespace.
+all its aspects including subject selection, data  selection and modification, data summary and 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1600: trailing whitespace.
+tabulation, estimation and hypothesis testing. Once data validation is complete, the analysis 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1601: trailing whitespace.
+should proceed according to the pre-defined plans; the more these plans are adhered to, the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1602: trailing whitespace.
+greater the credibility of the results. Particul ar attention should be paid to any differences 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1603: trailing whitespace.
+between the planned analysis and the actual analysis as described in the protocol, protocol 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1604: trailing whitespace.
+amendments or the updated statistical analysis plan based on a blind review of data. A careful 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1605: trailing whitespace.
+explanation should be provided for deviations from the planned analysis. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1606: trailing whitespace.
+All subjects who entered the trial should be accounted for in the report, whether or not they 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1607: trailing whitespace.
+are included in the analysis. All reasons for exclusion from analysis should be documented; 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1608: trailing whitespace.
+for any subject included in the full analysis set but not in the per protocol set, the reasons for 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1609: trailing whitespace.
+exclusion from the latter should also be documented. Similarly, for all subjects included in an 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1610: trailing whitespace.
+analysis set, the measurements of all important variables should be accounted for at all 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1611: trailing whitespace.
+relevant time-points. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1612: trailing whitespace.
+The effect of all losses of subjects or data , withdrawals from treatment and major protocol 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1613: trailing whitespace.
+violations on the main analyses of the primar y variable(s) should be considered carefully. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1614: trailing whitespace.
+Subjects lost to follow up, withdrawn from treatment, or with a severe protocol violation 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1615: trailing whitespace.
+should be identified, and a descriptive analysis of them provided, including the reasons for 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1616: trailing whitespace.
+their loss and its relationship to treatment and outcome. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1617: trailing whitespace.
+Descriptive statistics form an indispensable part of reports. Suitable tables and/or graphical 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1618: trailing whitespace.
+presentations should illustrate clearly the impor tant features of the primary and secondary 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1619: trailing whitespace.
+variables and of key prognostic and demographic variables. The results of the main analyses 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1620: trailing whitespace.
+relating to the objectives of the trial should be the subject of particularly careful descriptive 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1621: trailing whitespace.
+presentation. When reporting the results of significance tests, precise p-values (e.g.'p=0.034') 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1622: trailing whitespace.
+should be reported rather than making exclusive reference to critical values. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1623: trailing whitespace.
+Although the primary goal of the analysis of a cl inical trial should be to answer the questions 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1624: trailing whitespace.
+posed by its main objectives, new questions based on the observed data may well emerge 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1625: trailing whitespace.
+during the unblinded analysis. Additional and perhaps complex statistical analysis may be the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1626: trailing whitespace.
+consequence. This additional work should be st rictly distinguished in the report from work 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1627: trailing whitespace.
+which was planned in the protocol. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1631: trailing whitespace.
+Â© EMEA 2006  33 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1632: trailing whitespace.
+ 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1633: trailing whitespace.
+The play of chance may lead to unforeseen imbalances between the treatment groups in terms 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1634: trailing whitespace.
+of baseline measurements not pre-defined as covariates in the planned analysis but having 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1635: trailing whitespace.
+some prognostic importance nevertheless. This is best dealt with by showing that an 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1636: trailing whitespace.
+additional analysis which accounts for thes e imbalances reaches essentially the same 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1637: trailing whitespace.
+conclusions as the planned analysis. If this is not the case, the effect of the imbalances on the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1638: trailing whitespace.
+conclusions should be discussed.  
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1639: trailing whitespace.
+In general, sparing use should be made of unplanned analyses. Such analyses are often carried 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1640: trailing whitespace.
+out when it is thought that the treatment effect may vary according to some other factor or 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1641: trailing whitespace.
+factors. An attempt may then be made to identify subgroups of subjects for whom the effect is 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1642: trailing whitespace.
+particularly beneficial. The potential dangers  of over-interpretation of unplanned subgroup 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1643: trailing whitespace.
+analyses are well known (see also Section 5.7), and should be carefully avoided. Although 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1644: trailing whitespace.
+similar problems of interpretation arise if a treatment appears to have no benefit, or an adverse 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1645: trailing whitespace.
+effect, in a subgroup of subjects, such possibili ties should be properly assessed and should 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1646: trailing whitespace.
+therefore be reported.  
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1647: trailing whitespace.
+Finally statistical judgement should be brought to bear on the analysis, interpretation and 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1648: trailing whitespace.
+presentation of the results of a clinical trial. To this end the trial statistician should be a 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1649: trailing whitespace.
+member of the team responsible for the clinical  study report, and should approve the clinical 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1650: trailing whitespace.
+report.  
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1651: trailing whitespace.
+7.2 Summarising the Clinical Database 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1652: trailing whitespace.
+An overall summary and synthesis of the evidence on safety and efficacy from all the reported 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1653: trailing whitespace.
+clinical trials is required for a marketing application (Expert report in EU, integrated summary 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1654: trailing whitespace.
+reports in USA, Gaiyo in Japan). This may be ac companied, when appropriate, by a statistical 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1655: trailing whitespace.
+combination of results. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1656: trailing whitespace.
+Within the summary a number of areas of specific statistical interest arise: describing the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1657: trailing whitespace.
+demography and clinical features of the popula tion treated during the course of the clinical 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1658: trailing whitespace.
+trial programme; addressing the key questions of  efficacy by considering the results of the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1659: trailing whitespace.
+relevant (usually controlled) trials and high lighting the degree to which they reinforce or 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1660: trailing whitespace.
+contradict each other; summarising the safe ty information available from the combined 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1661: trailing whitespace.
+database of all the trials whose results contribute to the marketing application and identifying 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1662: trailing whitespace.
+potential safety issues. During the design of a clinical programme careful attention should be 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1663: trailing whitespace.
+paid to the uniform definition and collection of measurements which will facilitate subsequent 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1664: trailing whitespace.
+interpretation of the series of trials, particularly if they are likely to be combined across trials. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1665: trailing whitespace.
+A common dictionary for recording the details of medication, medical history and adverse 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1666: trailing whitespace.
+events should be selected and used. A common definition of the primary and secondary 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1667: trailing whitespace.
+variables is nearly always worthwhile, and essential for meta-analysis. The manner of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1668: trailing whitespace.
+measuring key efficacy variables, the timing of  assessments relative to randomisation/entry, 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1669: trailing whitespace.
+the handling of protocol violators and devi ators and perhaps the definition of prognostic 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1670: trailing whitespace.
+factors, should all be kept compatible unless there are valid reasons not to do so.  
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1671: trailing whitespace.
+Any statistical procedures used to combine data  across trials should be  described in detail. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1672: trailing whitespace.
+Attention should be paid to the possibility of bias  associated with the selection of trials, to the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1673: trailing whitespace.
+homogeneity of their results, and to the proper mo delling of the various sources of variation. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1674: trailing whitespace.
+The sensitivity of conclusions to the assumptions and selections made should be explored. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1675: trailing whitespace.
+7.2.1 Efficacy Data 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1676: trailing whitespace.
+Individual clinical trials should always be large enough to satisfy their objectives. Additional 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1677: trailing whitespace.
+valuable information may also be gained by summarising a series of clinical trials which 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1678: trailing whitespace.
+address essentially identical key efficacy questions. The main results of such a set of trials 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1679: trailing whitespace.
+should be presented in an identical form to pe rmit comparison, usually in tables or graphs 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1683: trailing whitespace.
+Â© EMEA 2006  34 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1684: trailing whitespace.
+ 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1685: trailing whitespace.
+which focus on estimates plus confidence limits. The use of meta-analytic techniques to 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1686: trailing whitespace.
+combine these estimates is often a useful addi tion, because it allows a more precise overall 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1687: trailing whitespace.
+estimate of the size of the treatment effects to be generated, and provides a complete and 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1688: trailing whitespace.
+concise summary of the results of the trials. Under exceptional circumstances a meta analytic 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1689: trailing whitespace.
+approach may also be the most appropriate way, or the only way, of providing sufficient 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1690: trailing whitespace.
+overall evidence of efficacy via an overall hypothesis test. When used for this purpose the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1691: trailing whitespace.
+meta-analysis should have its own prospectively written protocol. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1692: trailing whitespace.
+7.2.2   Safety Data 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1693: trailing whitespace.
+In summarising safety data it is important to examine the safety database thoroughly for any 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1694: trailing whitespace.
+indications of potential toxicity, and to follow up any indications by looking for an associated 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1695: trailing whitespace.
+supportive pattern of observations. The combination of the safety data from all human 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1696: trailing whitespace.
+exposure to the drug provides an important source  of information, because its larger sample 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1697: trailing whitespace.
+size provides the best chance of detecting the rarer adverse events and, perhaps, of estimating 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1698: trailing whitespace.
+their approximate incidence. However, incidence data from this database are difficult to 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1699: trailing whitespace.
+evaluate because of the lack of a comparator  group, and data from comparative trials are 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1700: trailing whitespace.
+especially valuable in overcoming this difficulty. The results from trials which use a common 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1701: trailing whitespace.
+comparator (placebo or specific active comparator) should be combined and presented 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1702: trailing whitespace.
+separately for each comparator providing sufficient data. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1703: trailing whitespace.
+All indications of potential toxicity arising fro m exploration of the data should be reported. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1704: trailing whitespace.
+The evaluation of the reality of these potential adverse effects should take account of the issue 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1705: trailing whitespace.
+of multiplicity arising from the numerous comparisons made. The evaluation should also 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1706: trailing whitespace.
+make appropriate use of survival analysis methods to exploit the potential relationship of the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1707: trailing whitespace.
+incidence of adverse events to duration of exposure and/or follow-up. The risks associated 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1708: trailing whitespace.
+with identified adverse effects should be appropriately quantified to allow a proper 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1709: trailing whitespace.
+assessment of the risk/benefit relationship. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1713: trailing whitespace.
+Â© EMEA 2006  35 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1714: trailing whitespace.
+ 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1715: trailing whitespace.
+GLOSSARY  
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1716: trailing whitespace.
+Bayesian Approaches 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1717: trailing whitespace.
+Approaches to data analysis that provide a posterior probability distribution for some 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1718: trailing whitespace.
+parameter (e.g. treatment eff ect), derived from the observed data and a prior probability 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1719: trailing whitespace.
+distribution for the parameter. The posterior distribution is then used as the basis for statistical 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1720: trailing whitespace.
+inference. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1721: trailing whitespace.
+Bias (Statistical & Operational) 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1722: trailing whitespace.
+The systematic tendency of any factors associ ated with the design, conduct, analysis and 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1723: trailing whitespace.
+evaluation of the results of a clinical trial to make the estimate of a treatment effect deviate 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1724: trailing whitespace.
+from its true value. Bias introduced through deviations in conduct is referred to as 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1725: trailing whitespace.
+'operational' bias. The other sources of bias listed above are referred to as 'statistical'. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1726: trailing whitespace.
+Blind Review 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1727: trailing whitespace.
+The checking and assessment of data during the period of time between trial completion (the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1728: trailing whitespace.
+last observation on the last subject) and the break ing of the blind, for the purpose of finalising 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1729: trailing whitespace.
+the planned analysis. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1730: trailing whitespace.
+Content Validity 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1731: trailing whitespace.
+The extent to which a variable (e.g. a rating scale) measures what it is supposed to measure. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1732: trailing whitespace.
+Double-Dummy 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1733: trailing whitespace.
+A technique for retaining the blind when administ ering supplies in a clinical trial, when the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1734: trailing whitespace.
+two treatments cannot be made identical. Supplies are prepared for Treatment A (active and 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1735: trailing whitespace.
+indistinguishable placebo) and for Treatmen t B (active and indistinguishable placebo). 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1736: trailing whitespace.
+Subjects then take two sets of treatment; either A (active) and B (placebo), or A (placebo) and 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1737: trailing whitespace.
+B (active). 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1738: trailing whitespace.
+Dropout 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1739: trailing whitespace.
+A subject in a clinical trial who for any reason fails to continue in the trial until the last visit 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1740: trailing whitespace.
+required of him/her by the study protocol. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1741: trailing whitespace.
+Equivalence Trial 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1742: trailing whitespace.
+A trial with the primary objective of showing that the response to two or more treatments 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1743: trailing whitespace.
+differs by an amount which is clinically unimportant. This is usually demonstrated by 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1744: trailing whitespace.
+showing that the true treatment difference is likely to lie between a lower and an upper 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1745: trailing whitespace.
+equivalence margin of clinically acceptable differences. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1746: trailing whitespace.
+Frequentist Methods 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1747: trailing whitespace.
+Statistical methods, such as significance test s and confidence intervals, which can be 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1748: trailing whitespace.
+interpreted in terms of the frequency of cert ain outcomes occurring in hypothetical repeated 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1749: trailing whitespace.
+realisations of the same experimental situation. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1750: trailing whitespace.
+Full Analysis Set 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1751: trailing whitespace.
+The set of subjects that is as close as possible to the ideal implied by the intention-to-treat 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1752: trailing whitespace.
+principle. It is derived from the set of all randomised subjects by minimal and justified 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1753: trailing whitespace.
+elimination of subjects. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1757: trailing whitespace.
+Â© EMEA 2006  36 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1758: trailing whitespace.
+ 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1759: trailing whitespace.
+Generalisability, Generalisation 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1760: trailing whitespace.
+The extent to which the findings of a clinical trial can be reliably extrapolated from the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1761: trailing whitespace.
+subjects who participated in the trial to a br oader patient population and a broader range of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1762: trailing whitespace.
+clinical settings. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1763: trailing whitespace.
+Global Assessment Variable 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1764: trailing whitespace.
+A single variable, usually a scale of ordered ca tegorical ratings, which integrates objective 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1765: trailing whitespace.
+variables and the investigator's overall impre ssion about the state or change in state of a 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1766: trailing whitespace.
+subject. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1767: trailing whitespace.
+Independent Data Monitoring  Committee (IDMC) (Data and Safety Monitoring Board, 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1768: trailing whitespace.
+Monitoring Committee, Data Monitoring Committee) 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1769: trailing whitespace.
+An independent data-monitoring committee that may be established by the sponsor to assess 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1770: trailing whitespace.
+at intervals the progress of a clin ical trial, the safety data, and the critical efficacy endpoints, 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1771: trailing whitespace.
+and to recommend to the sponsor whether to continue, modify, or stop a trial. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1772: trailing whitespace.
+Intention-To-Treat Principle 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1773: trailing whitespace.
+The principle that asserts that the effect of a treatment policy can be best assessed by 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1774: trailing whitespace.
+evaluating on the basis of the intention to trea t a subject (i.e. the planned treatment regimen) 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1775: trailing whitespace.
+rather than the actual treatment given. It has the consequence that subjects allocated to a 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1776: trailing whitespace.
+treatment group should be followed up, asse ssed and analysed as members of that group 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1777: trailing whitespace.
+irrespective of their compliance to the planned course of treatment. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1778: trailing whitespace.
+Interaction (Qualitative & Quantitative) 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1779: trailing whitespace.
+The situation in which a treatment contrast (e .g. difference between investigational product 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1780: trailing whitespace.
+and control) is dependent on another factor (e.g. centre). A quantitative interaction refers to 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1781: trailing whitespace.
+the case where the magnitude of the contrast differs at the different levels of the factor, 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1782: trailing whitespace.
+whereas for a qualitative interaction the direction of the contrast differs for at least one level 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1783: trailing whitespace.
+of the factor. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1784: trailing whitespace.
+Inter-Rater Reliability 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1785: trailing whitespace.
+The property of yielding equivalent results wh en used by different raters on different 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1786: trailing whitespace.
+occasions. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1787: trailing whitespace.
+Intra-Rater Reliability 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1788: trailing whitespace.
+The property of yielding equivalent results wh en used by the same rater on different 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1789: trailing whitespace.
+occasions. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1790: trailing whitespace.
+Interim Analysis 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1791: trailing whitespace.
+Any analysis intended to compare treatment arms with respect to efficacy or safety at any 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1792: trailing whitespace.
+time prior to the formal completion of a trial. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1793: trailing whitespace.
+Meta-Analysis 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1794: trailing whitespace.
+The formal evaluation of the quantitative evidence from two or more trials bearing on the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1795: trailing whitespace.
+same question. This most commonly involves the statistical combination of summary 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1796: trailing whitespace.
+statistics from the various trials, but the term is sometimes also used to refer to the 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1797: trailing whitespace.
+combination of the raw data. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1798: trailing whitespace.
+Multicentre Trial 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1799: trailing whitespace.
+A clinical trial conducted according to a single protocol but at more than one site, and 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1800: trailing whitespace.
+therefore, carried out by more than one investigator. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1801: trailing whitespace.
+ 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1805: trailing whitespace.
+Â© EMEA 2006  37 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1806: trailing whitespace.
+ 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1807: trailing whitespace.
+Non-Inferiority Trial 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1808: trailing whitespace.
+A trial with the primary objective of showing that  the response to the investigational product 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1809: trailing whitespace.
+is not clinically inferior to a comparative agent (active or placebo control). 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1810: trailing whitespace.
+Preferred and Included Terms 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1811: trailing whitespace.
+In a hierarchical medical dictionary, for example MedDRA, the included term is the lowest 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1812: trailing whitespace.
+level of dictionary term to which the investigat or description is coded. The preferred term is 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1813: trailing whitespace.
+the level of grouping of included terms typically used in reporting frequency of occurrence. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1814: trailing whitespace.
+For example, the investigator text â€œPain in the left armâ€ might be coded to the included term 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1815: trailing whitespace.
+â€œJoint painâ€, which is reported at the preferred term level as â€œArthralgiaâ€. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1816: trailing whitespace.
+Per Protocol Set (Valid Cases, Efficacy Sample, Evaluable Subjects Sample) 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1817: trailing whitespace.
+The set of data generated by the subset of  subjects who complied with the protocol 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1818: trailing whitespace.
+sufficiently to ensure that these data would be likely to exhibit the effects of treatment, 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1819: trailing whitespace.
+according to the underlying scientific model. Compliance covers such considerations as 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1820: trailing whitespace.
+exposure to treatment, availability of measurements and absence of major protocol violations. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1821: trailing whitespace.
+Safety & Tolerability 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1822: trailing whitespace.
+The safety of a medical product concerns the medical risk to the subject, usually assessed in a 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1823: trailing whitespace.
+clinical trial by laboratory tests (including c linical chemistry and haematology), vital signs, 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1824: trailing whitespace.
+clinical adverse events (diseases, signs and sy mptoms), and other special safety tests (e.g. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1825: trailing whitespace.
+ECGs, ophthalmology). The tolerability of the medical product represents the degree to which 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1826: trailing whitespace.
+overt adverse effects can be tolerated by the subject. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1827: trailing whitespace.
+Statistical Analysis Plan 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1828: trailing whitespace.
+A statistical analysis plan is a document that contains a more technical and detailed 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1829: trailing whitespace.
+elaboration of the principal features of the an alysis described in the protocol, and includes 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1830: trailing whitespace.
+detailed procedures for executing the statisti cal analysis of the primary and secondary 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1831: trailing whitespace.
+variables and other data. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1832: trailing whitespace.
+Superiority Trial 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1833: trailing whitespace.
+A trial with the primary objective of showing that  the response to the investigational product 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1834: trailing whitespace.
+is superior to a comparative agent (active or placebo control). 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1835: trailing whitespace.
+Surrogate Variable 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1836: trailing whitespace.
+A variable that provides an indirect measurement of effect in situations where direct 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1837: trailing whitespace.
+measurement of clinical effect is not feasible or practical. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1838: trailing whitespace.
+Treatment Effect 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1839: trailing whitespace.
+An effect attributed to a treatment in a clinical trial. In most clinical trials the treatment effect 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1840: trailing whitespace.
+of interest is a comparison (or contrast) of two or more treatments. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1841: trailing whitespace.
+Treatment Emergent 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1842: trailing whitespace.
+An event that emerges during treatment having been  absent pre-treatment, or worsens relative 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1843: trailing whitespace.
+to the pre-treatment state. 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1844: trailing whitespace.
+Trial Statistician 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1845: trailing whitespace.
+A statistician who has a combination of educ ation/training and experience sufficient to 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1846: trailing whitespace.
+implement the principles in this guidance and w ho is responsible for the statistical aspects of 
papers/Statistical_Principles_for_Clinical_Trials_ICH_E9_1998.md:1847: trailing whitespace.
+the trial. 
papers/The_use_of_confidence_or_fiducial_limits_illustrated_in_the_case_of_the_binomial.md:36: trailing whitespace.
+THE USE OF CONFIDENCE OR FIDUCIAL LIMITS 
